import Foundation

enum PIAQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "Northwind Logistics has a 15-year-old EDI-based warehouse system that communicates with partners over AS2, and a newer REST-based TMS. The integration architect must connect both to Salesforce Order Management without altering the warehouse system. Which approach best fits the existing landscape's protocol constraints?", options: [("A", "Expose a custom Apex REST endpoint and require the warehouse system to be rewritten to call it directly"), ("B", "Introduce middleware that translates AS2/EDI into REST/JSON before forwarding to Salesforce"), ("C", "Use Salesforce Outbound Messaging (SOAP) to poll the warehouse system every five minutes"), ("D", "Configure a Named Credential on the warehouse system to call the Salesforce Bulk API directly")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Middleware acting as a protocol bridge lets the legacy AS2/EDI system remain untouched while normalizing data into a format Salesforce can consume, which is the standard approach when legacy protocol constraints can't be changed. Why A fails: Rewriting the legacy system violates the stated constraint of not altering it and is not a realistic near-term option. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging pushes data *from* Salesforce on record changes; it can't poll or consume an external EDI feed. Why D fails: AS2/EDI systems don't natively speak Salesforce API protocols; a Named Credential doesn't perform format translation."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A hospital system runs a 20-year-old lab results feed using flat-file exports dropped nightly to an FTP server. The business now wants near-real-time result delivery to Salesforce Health Cloud. What is the most accurate assessment of this landscape's constraint?", options: [("A", "The flat-file/FTP mechanism is inherently synchronous and can be replaced with a direct Apex callout from the lab system"), ("B", "The flat-file/FTP mechanism is batch-oriented, so achieving near-real-time delivery requires replacing the file-drop with an event-based or API-based feed from the lab system itself"), ("C", "Salesforce Connect can virtualize the flat files in real time without any changes to the lab system"), ("D", "Platform Events can subscribe directly to the FTP server to receive lab results as they are created")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A nightly flat-file export is a batch pattern by design; near-real-time requirements can only be met by changing the source system's delivery mechanism to something event- or API-driven — middleware alone can't manufacture real-time data from a batch source. Why A fails: FTP file drops are not callouts; the lab system has no Apex or HTTP relationship with Salesforce. Why C fails: Salesforce Connect virtualizes queryable data sources (OData/custom adapters), not arbitrary flat files sitting on an FTP server. Why D fails: Platform Events are a Salesforce pub/sub mechanism; they cannot \"subscribe\" to an FTP server, which has no event-publishing capability."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "An architect reviewing a retailer's landscape finds that \"Customer\" is represented differently in the ERP (single name field), the e-commerce platform (first/last split with a middle name), and Salesforce (first/last/salutation). What should the architect document as the landscape finding?", options: [("A", "A canonical customer data model is needed, with transformation logic to map each system's representation to/from the canonical form"), ("B", "Salesforce should be modified to match the ERP's single-name-field structure to avoid transformation"), ("C", "No transformation is required as long as REST is used for all three systems"), ("D", "The e-commerce platform should be decommissioned since it has the most complex data structure")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: When multiple systems model the same entity differently, a canonical model with explicit field-level transformation (typically in middleware) is the standard way to reconcile format mismatches without forcing any one system to change its native model. Why B fails: Changing Salesforce's data model to match one legacy system just moves the mismatch to the other two systems and ignores Salesforce's own data model requirements. Why C fails: Transport protocol (REST) is unrelated to data shape; using REST everywhere does nothing to resolve field-mapping mismatches. Why D fails: Decommissioning a system is a business decision unrelated to data model complexity and isn't an architecture \"landscape finding.\""),
        Question(id: "4", question: "A field technician's mobile app must reserve inventory in an external warehouse system before completing a Salesforce work order, and the UI must show the technician confirmed availability before proceeding. Which integration boundary does this requirement mandate?", options: [("A", "Asynchronous, using Platform Events with no response requirement"), ("B", "Synchronous, request-reply, since the UI flow depends on an immediate confirmation before proceeding"), ("C", "Batch, nightly reconciliation between Salesforce and the warehouse system"), ("D", "Fire-and-forget, since inventory reservation doesn't need confirmation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Because the business process cannot proceed until the reservation is confirmed, this is a textbook synchronous request-reply boundary — the UI needs an immediate, blocking answer. Why A fails: Async events don't return an immediate confirmation the UI can act on before the technician proceeds. Why C fails: Nightly batch would let the technician proceed on stale data, directly violating the \"confirm before proceeding\" requirement. Why D fails: Fire-and-forget assumes no response is needed, but the scenario explicitly requires confirmation of availability."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "A financial services client's core banking system sits inside a private data center behind a firewall with no inbound internet access permitted by security policy. Salesforce needs updated account balances every few minutes. Which approach respects this network boundary?", options: [("A", "Configure a Salesforce Named Credential pointing directly at the core banking system's internal IP address"), ("B", "Have the core banking system (or an on-premise middleware agent) periodically push or poll data outward to Salesforce, since inbound connections to the data center are not permitted"), ("C", "Open an inbound firewall port on the core banking system so Salesforce can call it directly"), ("D", "Use Salesforce Connect to query the core banking system as an External Object over the public internet")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: When inbound access to a data center is disallowed, the integration must originate from inside the perimeter outward (on-prem agent/middleware pushing or polling out to Salesforce), which respects the security boundary while still meeting the business need. Why A fails: Salesforce cannot resolve or route to a private internal IP with no exposed public endpoint; Named Credentials don't bypass network firewalls. Why C fails: Opening an inbound port contradicts the stated security policy prohibiting inbound access. Why D fails: Salesforce Connect still requires an accessible external endpoint; it cannot reach into a network with no inbound access."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "A team wants to build a real-time Apex trigger that calls an external pricing API synchronously for every line item saved, expecting up to 5,000 line items in a single bulk data load. What platform limit consideration is most critical here?", options: [("A", "The 100-callout-per-transaction limit, since a bulk load could easily exceed available synchronous callouts in a single trigger execution"), ("B", "The 15,000-character formula field limit, since pricing formulas may be complex"), ("C", "The 6 MB email attachment limit, since pricing responses may include attachments"), ("D", "The 2 GB org data storage limit, since API responses consume storage")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Apex enforces a maximum of 100 external callouts per transaction; a bulk load of 5,000 records processed synchronously in a trigger would far exceed this, forcing an asynchronous (Queueable/Batch) redesign. Why B fails: Formula field character limits are unrelated to callout volume or transaction-level API integration limits. Why C fails: Email attachment limits have nothing to do with synchronous API callouts from a trigger. Why D fails: Data storage limits govern record storage, not the number of callouts permitted per transaction."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "A global enterprise operates separate Salesforce orgs for EMEA and APAC, plus a Government Cloud org for a public-sector subsidiary. The architect must design a single integration pattern to sync product catalog data to all three. What org topology constraint most affects the design?", options: [("A", "Government Cloud orgs cannot participate in any inbound or outbound integration"), ("B", "All three orgs share a single combined API limit pool, so total request volume is unconstrained"), ("C", "Government Cloud has separate compliance and network-isolation requirements (e.g., FedRAMP boundaries), so the integration must be designed per-org rather than assuming uniform connectivity/security across all three"), ("D", "Multi-org topologies always require Salesforce-to-Salesforce (SF2SF) as the only valid integration mechanism")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why C: Government Cloud instances operate under distinct compliance regimes (e.g., FedRAMP) and network boundaries, meaning the integration architecture and security controls typically must be designed and validated separately for that org rather than treated identically to commercial orgs. Why A fails: Government Cloud orgs do support integrations; they just carry additional compliance and connectivity requirements, not a blanket prohibition. Why B fails: Each Salesforce org has its own independent API limit allocation — they are not pooled across orgs. Why D fails: SF2SF is one option for org-to-org integration, not a \"required\" or exclusive mechanism; middleware or APIs are equally valid."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "An architect is designing authentication for a nightly batch job that runs unattended on a server to push data into Salesforce with no human present to approve a login. Which OAuth consideration should drive the authentication design?", options: [("A", "A user-context, browser-redirect flow, since it provides the strongest security for unattended jobs"), ("B", "A flow that supports server-to-server authentication without user interaction, such as JWT Bearer, since no user is present to approve access"), ("C", "SAML SSO federation through the corporate identity provider, since it's required for all integrations"), ("D", "A shared personal user's username and password hardcoded in the batch script")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Unattended, headless processes need an OAuth flow that doesn't require interactive user consent; the JWT Bearer flow (or Client Credentials) is designed exactly for this server-to-server case. Why A fails: Browser-redirect (Web Server) flow requires a live user session to approve access, which is unavailable in an unattended batch job. Why C fails: SAML SSO is for federated human user authentication to applications, not a requirement for all machine-to-machine integrations. Why D fails: Hardcoded username/password credentials are a security anti-pattern and don't leverage OAuth token-based security at all."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A retailer has 12 point-to-point integrations directly wired between Salesforce and various backend systems. Every time a backend system changes its API, multiple point-to-point integrations break. What is the best architectural response to this landscape pain point?", options: [("A", "Add more point-to-point integrations to increase redundancy"), ("B", "Introduce a middleware/ESB layer to decouple systems so backend changes are absorbed centrally rather than propagating to every consumer"), ("C", "Migrate all point-to-point integrations to Salesforce Outbound Messaging, since it is more resilient by design"), ("D", "Consolidate all 12 integrations into a single Apex class to simplify maintenance")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A middleware/ESB layer decouples producers and consumers, so a backend API change only requires updating the middleware adapter, not every downstream point-to-point consumer — this directly resolves brittle tight coupling. Why A fails: More point-to-point connections increase, not reduce, the coupling and fragility the scenario describes. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging is a specific SOAP-based notification mechanism, not inherently more resilient to backend API changes, and doesn't address the coupling problem. Why D fails: Consolidating logic into one Apex class doesn't remove the tight coupling between each backend system and Salesforce; it just centralizes the same brittle wiring."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "An architect finds that customer address data is updated independently in Salesforce, the ERP, and the billing system, causing conflicting values across systems. What is the correct architectural response to this pain point?", options: [("A", "Increase the polling frequency between all three systems so conflicts resolve faster"), ("B", "Designate one system as the authoritative system of record for address data, and route all updates through it or synchronize with conflict-resolution rules based on that authority"), ("C", "Disable address editing in Salesforce only, leaving ERP and billing unrestricted"), ("D", "Store three separate address fields per system in Salesforce and let users manually reconcile them")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The root cause of the conflict is the lack of a defined system of record; establishing authoritative ownership per data domain (with matching sync/conflict rules) is the standard fix for multi-system data duplication conflicts. Why A fails: Faster polling doesn't resolve *which* value is correct when two systems disagree — it just propagates conflicts faster. Why C fails: Restricting only one system doesn't address that ERP and billing can still independently create conflicting updates. Why D fails: Storing three parallel fields and relying on manual reconciliation doesn't scale and doesn't solve the underlying system-of-record ambiguity."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "A company's Salesforce org already runs three integrations that together consume 70% of the daily API call allocation. A new marketing integration wants to add a fourth polling-based sync every minute. What should the architect flag as the key constraint?", options: [("A", "The daily API limit is shared org-wide across all integrations, so a new high-frequency polling integration risks exhausting the allocation and should consider event-driven alternatives (e.g., Platform Events/CDC) instead of frequent polling"), ("B", "Each Connected App gets its own independent daily API allocation, so the new integration will not affect the existing three"), ("C", "Polling every minute has no measurable effect on API consumption since polling calls are free"), ("D", "The 100,000-record Bulk API limit applies here and prevents the new integration from being built at all")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Salesforce daily API limits are pooled per org, not per Connected App, so a new frequent-polling integration competes for the same shrinking allocation; switching to event-driven delivery (CDC/Platform Events) avoids repeated polling calls entirely. Why B fails: Connected Apps do not each get a separate API allocation — the daily limit is enforced at the org level. Why C fails: Every REST/SOAP API call, including polling calls, consumes the org's API limit; polling is not exempt. Why D fails: The Bulk API record limit is unrelated to daily API call limits and doesn't categorically block building the integration."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A manufacturer's MES (Manufacturing Execution System) runs entirely on-premise with no public endpoint and strict IT policy against exposing it to the internet. Salesforce Field Service needs work order status updates from the MES. Which hosting constraint should drive the integration design?", options: [("A", "Since the MES has no public endpoint and cannot be exposed, a hybrid approach with an on-premise middleware runtime (e.g., an on-prem Mule runtime or gateway) that securely relays data outward to Salesforce is required"), ("B", "Salesforce Connect can query the on-premise MES directly with no additional infrastructure"), ("C", "The MES should be migrated to Heroku Postgres so Heroku Connect can sync it directly"), ("D", "A Named Credential in Salesforce can reach the MES because Named Credentials bypass network restrictions")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: When a source system cannot be exposed externally, an on-premise middleware/gateway component is needed to securely bridge the internal network to the cloud, which is the standard hybrid deployment pattern for this constraint. Why B fails: Salesforce Connect adapters still need a reachable endpoint; they cannot query a system with no exposed network path. Why C fails: Migrating the entire MES to a new database platform is a disproportionate, unstated solution to what is fundamentally a network exposure/connectivity constraint. Why D fails: Named Credentials manage authentication for reachable endpoints; they don't grant network access through firewalls to unreachable internal systems."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "A stakeholder says: \"Order data must transform currency values before landing in Salesforce, and the whole sync must never take longer than 10 seconds end-to-end.\" Which correctly classifies these two requirements?", options: [("A", "Both are functional requirements, since they both describe system behavior"), ("B", "The currency transformation is a functional requirement; the 10-second timing constraint is a non-functional requirement (performance/latency SLA)"), ("C", "Both are non-functional requirements, since they both relate to integration design"), ("D", "The currency transformation is non-functional; the timing constraint is functional")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Functional requirements describe *what* the system must do (transform currency); non-functional requirements describe *quality attributes* like performance, latency, and availability — the 10-second SLA is a classic non-functional latency requirement. Why A fails: The timing constraint isn't about a business transformation rule; it's a quality-of-service constraint, which is the definition of non-functional. Why C fails: Currency transformation is a concrete business/data rule the integration must perform, which is functional by definition. Why D fails: This reverses the correct classification entirely."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A business requirement states that inventory levels in Salesforce must reflect warehouse changes within 15 minutes, but sub-minute latency is not required and the warehouse system cannot support event publishing. Which integration pattern selection best matches this SLA?", options: [("A", "Real-time synchronous request-reply on every warehouse transaction"), ("B", "Scheduled batch or micro-batch polling every 5–10 minutes, since it comfortably meets the 15-minute SLA without requiring event capability the source lacks"), ("C", "Platform Events published directly from the warehouse system"), ("D", "Change Data Capture on the warehouse system's database")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A 15-minute SLA doesn't require true real-time delivery, and since the source can't publish events, a scheduled polling/batch pattern at a shorter interval than the SLA threshold is the simplest solution that meets the requirement without unavailable capabilities. Why A fails: Synchronous request-reply per transaction is unnecessary overhead for a 15-minute tolerance and assumes tight coupling not required here. Why C fails: The scenario explicitly states the warehouse system cannot support event publishing, ruling out Platform Events as the source-side mechanism. Why D fails: CDC is a Salesforce feature for outbound change tracking, not something you can enable on a third-party warehouse system's database."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "An integration architect designs a retry mechanism for an order-creation callout that may occasionally time out even after the order was successfully created downstream. What non-functional requirement must be addressed to safely retry without creating duplicate orders?", options: [("A", "Idempotency — the integration must be designed so retrying the same request does not create a duplicate order (e.g., via an idempotency key or external ID upsert)"), ("B", "Elasticity — the system must automatically add more servers during retries"), ("C", "Encryption at rest — retried payloads must be encrypted differently than the original"), ("D", "Data classification — retried records must be reclassified as Confidential")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Idempotency ensures that repeating the same operation (e.g., due to a timeout-triggered retry) produces the same end state rather than duplicate records — exactly the risk described. Why B fails: Elasticity/auto-scaling addresses capacity under load, not the duplicate-record risk from retries. Why C fails: Encryption at rest is a data-protection concern unrelated to preventing duplicate order creation. Why D fails: Data classification governs security handling of data sensitivity, not duplicate-prevention logic for retried transactions."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "A business requires that if the external payment gateway is unreachable, in-flight Salesforce checkout transactions must not be lost and should automatically resume once the gateway recovers. What should the architect specify as a requirement category, and what design responds to it?", options: [("A", "A functional requirement, addressed by adding a validation rule on the Opportunity object"), ("B", "An availability/resilience non-functional requirement, addressed by queuing failed requests (e.g., via a durable queue or Platform Events with replay) and retrying automatically once the gateway is available"), ("C", "A data classification requirement, addressed by masking payment fields"), ("D", "A regulatory requirement, addressed by generating a compliance report")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Ensuring no transaction loss during downstream outages and automatic resumption is a resilience/availability non-functional requirement, best met with durable queuing and retry/replay mechanisms designed for exactly this failure mode. Why A fails: A validation rule enforces data integrity on save; it cannot queue or retry a failed external gateway call. Why C fails: Field masking addresses data sensitivity, not transaction durability during an outage. Why D fails: Compliance reporting is unrelated to ensuring in-flight transactions survive a gateway outage."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "During discovery, the business says integration failures currently go unnoticed for days because no one is alerted. What non-functional requirement is missing, and what should be designed in from the start?", options: [("A", "A scalability requirement, addressed by increasing batch size"), ("B", "An observability requirement, addressed by building logging, tracing, and proactive alerting into the integration from initial design, not added afterward"), ("C", "A data classification requirement, addressed by encrypting log files"), ("D", "A localization requirement, addressed by translating error messages")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The described gap — failures going unnoticed — is precisely an observability gap; the correct response is designing logging, tracing, and alerting as first-class requirements from the start, not bolting them on later. Why A fails: Batch size relates to throughput/scalability, not visibility into failures. Why C fails: Encrypting logs is a security control and does nothing to surface failures to operations staff. Why D fails: Translating error messages addresses localization, not the underlying lack of alerting."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A mid-size company has one part-time admin and no dedicated integration developers, but needs to integrate Salesforce with five SaaS applications with mostly standard connectors available. What maintainability factor should most influence the build vs. buy decision?", options: [("A", "Build custom Apex integrations for all five systems, since it gives maximum long-term flexibility regardless of team size"), ("B", "Favor a low-code/iPaaS or pre-built connector approach given the limited in-house integration skills and staffing, minimizing the ongoing custom-code maintenance burden"), ("C", "Ignore team skills entirely and choose based solely on per-transaction cost"), ("D", "Always choose the cheapest licensing option regardless of operational support model")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Maintainability requirements must account for available skills and support capacity; with limited developer resources, a low-code/iPaaS or connector-based approach reduces the long-term burden of maintaining custom code that no one on staff can easily support. Why A fails: Building custom Apex for five systems creates a maintenance burden the team explicitly lacks capacity for, ignoring the stated staffing constraint. Why C fails: Cost alone ignores the operational/maintainability requirement that is central to this scenario. Why D fails: Licensing cost without considering support model risk directly contradicts the maintainability factor the question asks about."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "A healthcare provider integration transmits patient diagnosis codes and treatment notes from Salesforce Health Cloud to a third-party analytics platform. How should this data be classified, and what does that classification require?", options: [("A", "Public data; no special handling is required since analytics platforms are trusted by default"), ("B", "Confidential/Secure (PHI); classification requires encryption in transit (TLS) and likely field-level protections such as masking/tokenization plus strict access controls, consistent with regulations like HIPAA"), ("C", "Secure data, but classification has no bearing on transport security choices"), ("D", "Public data, but transformation rules must still anonymize customer names only")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Diagnosis codes and treatment notes are Protected Health Information (PHI), which must be classified as Confidential/Secure — driving requirements like TLS in transit, field-level protection, and access controls in line with HIPAA. Why A fails: Treating PHI as Public data ignores its regulated, sensitive nature and the trust level of a third party is irrelevant to classification. Why C fails: Data classification directly determines transport security requirements (e.g., mandating TLS/mTLS); the two are not independent. Why D fails: PHI is not Public data, and anonymizing only names while leaving diagnosis/treatment details exposed does not meet PHI protection requirements."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "Given a classification of \"Confidential\" for financial transaction data flowing outbound from Salesforce to a partner bank, what transport-level requirement should the architect mandate?", options: [("A", "Standard HTTP is acceptable as long as the payload is compressed"), ("B", "TLS (and potentially mutual TLS for higher assurance) must be enforced for the callout, with certificate validation rather than disabling SSL/TLS verification"), ("C", "No transport security is needed since Named Credentials handle all security automatically"), ("D", "Basic authentication over HTTP is sufficient if the password is complex")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Confidential financial data being transmitted externally requires encryption in transit; enforcing TLS (and mTLS for higher-assurance partner integrations) with proper certificate validation is the correct transport security control for this classification. Why A fails: Plain HTTP transmits data unencrypted regardless of compression, exposing Confidential data to interception. Why C fails: Named Credentials manage authentication/credentials but do not automatically guarantee the endpoint enforces TLS; the architect must still mandate a secure endpoint configuration. Why D fails: Basic auth over unencrypted HTTP still exposes credentials and payload in transit; password complexity doesn't fix a transport security gap."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "An integration sends customer Social Security Numbers from Salesforce to a downstream fraud-detection vendor, but the vendor's own compliance team requires that Salesforce never send the raw SSN in transformation logs or middleware traces. What technique should the architect apply?", options: [("A", "Send the SSN in plaintext but restrict who can view integration logs after the fact"), ("B", "Apply tokenization or masking of the SSN field before it reaches middleware logging layers, so raw values never appear in logs or traces even if downstream processing needs a token equivalent"), ("C", "Store the SSN in a formula field so it can't be logged"), ("D", "Remove SSN from the payload entirely and hope the vendor doesn't need it")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Tokenization/masking replaces the sensitive raw value before it enters logging or trace layers, satisfying the requirement that raw SSNs never appear in logs while still allowing downstream systems to reference a token. Why A fails: Restricting log access after the fact still exposes the raw SSN in the log/trace pipeline itself, which is exactly what must be prevented. Why C fails: Formula fields are a Salesforce display mechanism; they don't control what a middleware layer captures in transformation logs. Why D fails: Simply omitting a required field breaks the integration's functional purpose rather than solving the logging exposure problem."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "European retailer's compliance team states that customer PII must not be persisted or processed by infrastructure located outside the EU. The company is evaluating a US-hosted iPaaS for Salesforce integrations. What should the architect flag?", options: [("A", "Data residency requirements from regulations like GDPR may preclude routing/persisting PII through non-EU-hosted middleware infrastructure, requiring an EU-region deployment or regional data center option"), ("B", "GDPR only applies to Salesforce itself, not to middleware, so the US-hosted iPaaS is automatically compliant"), ("C", "Data residency is only a concern for Bulk API jobs, not real-time integrations"), ("D", "As long as data is encrypted, its physical processing location is irrelevant under GDPR")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: GDPR-style data residency obligations can restrict where PII is processed or stored, not just where it's queried from; a US-hosted middleware region could violate this unless an EU-region deployment option is used. Why B fails: GDPR residency obligations apply to any processor handling the data, including middleware, not just the primary system of record. Why C fails: Residency requirements apply regardless of integration pattern (batch or real-time); the pattern doesn't change the legal residency obligation. Why D fails: Encryption addresses confidentiality, not the legal requirement about the physical/jurisdictional location of processing under many residency regulations."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A sales VP complains that reps can't see recent support tickets or shipping status while working an opportunity in Salesforce. What integration requirement should the architect capture as key to CRM success?", options: [("A", "A requirement to integrate the support and shipping systems' relevant data into Salesforce (or make it accessible in context) to give reps a unified 360-degree customer view"), ("B", "A requirement to train reps to check three separate systems manually before every call"), ("C", "A requirement to disable the Opportunity object until a full data warehouse is built"), ("D", "A requirement to migrate support tickets into a spreadsheet reps can email each other")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The complaint is a classic symptom of a fragmented customer view; integrating support and shipping data into (or accessible from) Salesforce is the direct requirement that restores the 360-degree view critical to CRM adoption. Why B fails: Manual cross-checking is the current painful workaround, not a requirement — the goal is to eliminate this manual effort. Why C fails: Disabling core CRM functionality contradicts the goal of improving CRM usability and provides no integration benefit. Why D fails: Emailing spreadsheets is an ad hoc, non-scalable workaround, not a legitimate integration requirement."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "Two systems (Salesforce and the billing platform) both allow editing of a customer's \"preferred contact method\" field, leading to conflicting values reps see in different tools. What requirement should be defined to prevent recurring CRM data conflicts?", options: [("A", "A requirement that both systems keep independent copies with no coordination, since reps can just ask the customer directly each time"), ("B", "A requirement establishing a clear system of record for \"preferred contact method,\" with the non-authoritative system reading (not writing) that field, or a defined sync/conflict-resolution rule"), ("C", "A requirement to delete the field from both systems"), ("D", "A requirement to only fix the issue in Salesforce, leaving the billing platform unrestricted")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The recurring conflict stems from ambiguous ownership; defining a single system of record (with the other system reading only, or a governed sync rule) is the standard requirement that prevents this class of CRM data conflict. Why A fails: Leaving both systems independently editable perpetuates the exact conflict described and adds manual customer-facing friction. Why C fails: Deleting the field removes needed functionality rather than resolving the ownership ambiguity. Why D fails: Restricting only one system while leaving the other free to edit still allows the billing platform to create conflicting values."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "Account records with inconsistent addresses fed in from three integrated source systems. What requirement should be added to the integration design to support CRM success?", options: [("A", "A data quality requirement covering deduplication rules, matching keys, and standardized field validation applied during integration/data load, not left to manual cleanup afterward"), ("B", "A requirement to disable the integration feeding the least active source system only"), ("C", "A requirement to increase the Salesforce Account object's field character limits"), ("D", "A requirement to give every rep edit access to merge duplicates manually at will")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Duplicate and inconsistent records are a classic data quality failure; the fix is a defined requirement for deduplication logic, matching keys (e.g., external IDs), and validation built into the integration pipeline itself. Why B fails: Disabling one source doesn't address root-cause data quality issues and likely creates data gaps. Why C fails: Field character limits are unrelated to duplicate detection or address consistency. Why D fails: Unrestricted manual merging by all reps, without governed matching rules, risks incorrect merges and doesn't scale as an integration requirement."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "Sales reps say that when they update a quote in Salesforce, the ERP-calculated tax amount often doesn't appear for several minutes, causing them to quote customers incorrect totals live on calls. What requirement should be captured?", options: [("A", "A batch nightly sync is sufficient since tax rarely changes"), ("B", "A near-real-time (or synchronous) integration requirement for tax calculation, since the rep-facing UX depends on seeing accurate totals during a live customer interaction"), ("C", "A requirement to remove tax display from the quote UI entirely"), ("D", "A requirement to have reps manually calculate tax using a spreadsheet")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Because the business impact is incorrect quotes given live to customers, the UX clearly requires near-real-time (or synchronous) tax calculation rather than a delayed batch update — this is a direct case of UX driving integration latency requirements. Why A fails: A nightly batch directly causes the described problem (stale totals during live calls); it does not resolve it. Why C fails: Removing the tax display eliminates the needed information rather than fixing its timeliness. Why D fails: Manual spreadsheet calculation is an unscalable workaround and doesn't address the underlying integration requirement."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A company is expanding from serving only US customers to also serving customers in Germany and Brazil, both of which have distinct data residency and cross-border transfer rules. What should the architect flag as a growth-driven integration requirement?", options: [("A", "No changes are needed since Salesforce automatically complies with all countries' data laws"), ("B", "The integration architecture must account for regional data residency and cross-border transfer restrictions (e.g., GDPR, Brazil's LGPD), potentially requiring regional data handling or restricted replication of certain fields"), ("C", "Only the UI language needs to change; data handling requirements are identical globally"), ("D", "Growth into new geographies only affects Salesforce license counts, not integration design")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Expanding into new regulatory jurisdictions like Germany (GDPR) and Brazil (LGPD) introduces distinct residency and cross-border transfer obligations that must be reflected in integration and data architecture, not just UI localization. Why A fails: Salesforce provides platform capabilities (e.g., Shield, data residency options) but doesn't automatically guarantee compliance — the architecture must still be designed to meet each jurisdiction's rules. Why C fails: Regulatory data handling requirements are substantive, not cosmetic, and go far beyond language localization. Why D fails: Licensing is a commercial concern unrelated to the legal/technical data handling requirements introduced by new geographies."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A healthcare payer must keep certain PHI-processing components within its own audited data center per regulatory requirements, but still wants to leverage Salesforce Health Cloud for case management. What integration deployment factor should this drive?", options: [("A", "A fully cloud-native iPaaS with no on-premise footprint should always be preferred to reduce operational overhead"), ("B", "A hybrid deployment model (e.g., middleware runtime deployed within the audited on-premise/private environment) may be required so PHI processing remains within the boundary mandated by regulation"), ("C", "Regulatory requirements never affect the choice between cloud and on-premise middleware"), ("D", "The company should stop using Salesforce Health Cloud since no integration can satisfy healthcare compliance rules")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: When regulation mandates that certain data processing remain within an audited environment, a hybrid architecture — keeping the regulated processing component on-premise while still integrating with cloud-based Salesforce — satisfies both the compliance boundary and the business goal. Why A fails: A fully cloud-native approach directly conflicts with the stated regulatory requirement to keep PHI processing within the audited data center. Why C fails: Industry-specific compliance mandates are precisely the kind of factor that does influence cloud vs. on-premise/hybrid middleware decisions. Why D fails: This is an extreme, unsupported conclusion — hybrid architectures exist specifically to reconcile cloud CRM adoption with regulatory data residency needs."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "M\\&A driving integration choice Following an acquisition, the parent company must rapidly connect the acquired company's separate CRM and ERP systems to its own Salesforce org within a tight 90-day integration window, with the acquired systems likely to be decommissioned within a year. What should most influence the integration solution choice?", options: [("A", "Building a fully custom, deeply optimized point-to-point Apex integration designed for a 10-year lifespan"), ("B", "Favoring fast-to-implement, loosely coupled integration options (e.g., pre-built connectors, iPaaS templates, or temporary data migration/sync jobs) given the short timeline and the systems' planned decommissioning"), ("C", "Ignoring the 90-day deadline and proposing a full enterprise data warehouse redesign first"), ("D", "Requiring the acquired company to migrate directly into Salesforce with no interim integration at all")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: M&A scenarios with tight timelines and short-lived source systems call for fast, low-investment integration approaches rather than heavy, long-lived custom builds, since the effort must be proportional to the systems' short remaining lifespan. Why A fails: Investing in a decade-long custom architecture for systems being decommissioned within a year is a poor use of effort and won't meet the 90-day deadline either. Why C fails: A full data warehouse redesign is a large, slow initiative that directly conflicts with the urgent 90-day business requirement. Why D fails: Skipping interim integration ignores the practical reality that direct migration of complex CRM/ERP data typically cannot be completed cleanly in 90 days without a bridging integration."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "An architect reviews a landscape diagram showing Salesforce directly wired to six backend systems, each with its own dedicated connection, no shared broker. What should the inventory note about topology?", options: [("A", "Hub-and-spoke topology, since Salesforce is a single central hub for six spokes"), ("B", "Point-to-point topology, since each connection is a dedicated link between two systems with no shared intermediary broker"), ("C", "Publish-subscribe topology, since Salesforce receives from six sources"), ("D", "Orchestration topology, since Salesforce coordinates six systems")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Point-to-point topology is defined by dedicated direct connections between pairs of systems with no shared broker/mediator — exactly what's described, even though Salesforce happens to be one end of every connection. Why A fails: Hub-and-spoke implies a broker or mediating hub routes/transforms messages between spokes; here there's no such mediation layer, just direct wiring. Why C fails: Pub-sub requires a topic/event broker decoupling publishers from subscribers, which isn't present in a direct-wire diagram. Why D fails: Orchestration implies a central process actively sequencing calls to multiple systems as part of a single business process, not just static direct connections."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "A landscape review reveals 20 point-to-point integrations that are becoming unmanageable as the company adds new systems. The team is deciding whether to move to a hub-and-spoke model using an integration platform. What operational trade-off should the architect highlight?", options: [("A", "Hub-and-spoke eliminates all latency since messages travel directly between systems"), ("B", "Hub-and-spoke centralizes transformation and routing logic, reducing the N² integration growth problem, at the cost of introducing a central component that must be highly available"), ("C", "Hub-and-spoke requires exactly the same number of connections as point-to-point, so there is no real benefit"), ("D", "Point-to-point is always superior because it has no single point of failure")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Hub-and-spoke reduces the combinatorial growth of direct connections by centralizing routing/transformation in a hub, but that hub becomes a critical dependency requiring high availability — a genuine architectural trade-off. Why A fails: Hub-and-spoke typically adds a hop through the hub, which can add latency compared to a direct point-to-point call, not eliminate it. Why C fails: Hub-and-spoke reduces the number of distinct connections needed as systems are added (each new system connects once to the hub), unlike point-to-point's combinatorial growth. Why D fails: Point-to-point avoids a single central point of failure but doesn't avoid the cascading-breakage problem described in the scenario, so calling it \"always superior\" ignores the stated pain point."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "A landscape diagram shows Salesforce calling a shipping label generation service and waiting for the generated label PDF URL before displaying it to the user. Which pattern is depicted, and what should the inventory note?", options: [("A", "Fire-and-forget, since Salesforce initiates the call"), ("B", "Request-reply, since Salesforce blocks and requires an immediate response (the label URL) before proceeding with the UI"), ("C", "Publish-subscribe, since a label is \"published\" to the user"), ("D", "Batch synchronization, since labels are generated periodically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The defining characteristic of request-reply is that the caller blocks and requires the response to proceed — exactly the case where Salesforce needs the label URL to update the UI immediately. Why A fails: Fire-and-forget means no response is expected or waited on; here Salesforce explicitly waits for the URL. Why C fails: Pub-sub involves a decoupled broker distributing events to multiple subscribers, not a direct synchronous call awaiting a specific reply. Why D fails: This is a real-time synchronous interaction, not a scheduled batch process."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A diagram shows a nightly job extracting all modified Accounts from an ERP and loading them into Salesforce via Bulk API, with no real-time trigger involved. Which pattern should be recorded in the inventory?", options: [("A", "Batch data synchronization pattern, since data moves on a scheduled interval rather than being triggered by individual events"), ("B", "Publish-subscribe pattern, since the ERP publishes accounts"), ("C", "Request-reply pattern, since Salesforce receives a reply from the ERP"), ("D", "Process choreography pattern, since multiple steps are involved")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A nightly, scheduled extraction and bulk load of changed records with no event trigger is the textbook definition of the batch data synchronization pattern. Why B fails: Pub-sub requires an event broker and near-real-time delivery triggered by individual changes, not a scheduled nightly extract. Why C fails: Request-reply describes a synchronous call/response interaction, not a scheduled bulk data movement. Why D fails: Process choreography describes multiple systems reacting to each other's events across a business process, not a single scheduled ETL job."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "CDC/Streaming/Platform Events identification A vendor's order management system needs to know whenever an Opportunity's Stage changes to \"Closed Won\" in Salesforce, without polling, and wants the full old/new field values in the notification. Which pattern/technology should the inventory identify as best fit?", options: [("A", "Outbound Messaging, since it is the only Salesforce feature that can notify external systems"), ("B", "Change Data Capture, since it publishes change events including changed field information without requiring the external system to poll"), ("C", "Scheduled Apex polling every minute, since it guarantees delivery"), ("D", "Salesforce Connect, since it virtualizes external data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Change Data Capture publishes structured change events (including changed field details) via the Streaming API whenever tracked records change, letting external subscribers react without polling — precisely matching the requirement. Why A fails: Outbound Messaging is workflow/process-based and typically sends a fixed SOAP payload; it's not the only notification mechanism, and CDC is generally the more modern fit for detailed field-level change data. Why C fails: Polling contradicts the explicit \"without polling\" requirement and adds unnecessary API consumption. Why D fails: Salesforce Connect surfaces external data as if it were Salesforce data; it doesn't push Salesforce changes outward."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Two systems in a landscape diagram are connected by a component that queries Salesforce's REST API every 60 seconds looking for new Leads. What should this be classified as, and what limitation should the architect note?", options: [("A", "An event-driven integration, which scales efficiently regardless of Lead volume"), ("B", "A polling-based integration, which consumes API calls on a fixed schedule regardless of whether new data exists, and introduces up to 60 seconds of latency"), ("C", "A Change Data Capture integration, since it retrieves changed records"), ("D", "A fire-and-forget integration, since no response is expected")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Querying on a fixed interval regardless of whether new data exists is the definition of polling; it wastes API calls on empty results and introduces latency bounded by the polling interval — both worth flagging. Why A fails: Event-driven integrations react only when something happens; a fixed-interval query is the opposite pattern. Why C fails: CDC is a specific Salesforce push-based streaming feature; a REST polling loop querying manually is not CDC, even if it retrieves changed records. Why D fails: Fire-and-forget describes not waiting for a response on an outbound call, unrelated to a polling loop that expects query results back."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "In a landscape diagram, an Order Placed event in Salesforce triggers the Shipping system to react and publish a Shipped event, which the Billing system then independently reacts to by generating an invoice — with no central controller directing these steps. What pattern is this?", options: [("A", "Orchestration, since multiple systems are involved in sequence"), ("B", "Process choreography, since each system reacts independently to events from others with no central coordinator"), ("C", "Request-reply, since each system waits for a reply"), ("D", "Batch synchronization, since events happen in order")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Choreography is characterized by each participant independently reacting to events without a central coordinator directing the sequence — exactly matching the decentralized event-reaction chain described. Why A fails: Orchestration implies a central process (e.g., a MuleSoft flow or orchestrator) explicitly sequencing and directing each step, which is absent here. Why C fails: Request-reply requires a synchronous blocking call awaiting a direct response, not independent asynchronous event reactions. Why D fails: Nothing here is on a fixed schedule; it's event-triggered reaction, not scheduled batch movement."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "SF2SF vs middleware for multi-org A landscape diagram shows two Salesforce orgs (a subsidiary and the parent) that need to share Opportunity and related Contact/Account records bidirectionally, including relationship data queried via SOQL joins on the receiving side. Which approach should the architect flag as more suitable, and why?", options: [("A", "Native Salesforce-to-Salesforce (SF2SF), since it's free and requires no additional licensing"), ("B", "Middleware-brokered integration, since SF2SF has historically had limitations with complex relationship fidelity, error handling, and it may be preferable when queries need full relational SOQL support on synchronized data across orgs"), ("C", "Salesforce Connect, since External Objects fully support SOQL relationship joins identical to native objects"), ("D", "Change Data Capture directly between two orgs with no other components")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: While SF2SF is simple for basic sharing, middleware brokering is often preferred for complex relational fidelity, custom error handling, and monitoring needs, especially when downstream SOQL relationship queries must behave like native data — a known trade-off area for this exam. Why A fails: Cost/licensing is not the primary architectural driver here; the question centers on relationship fidelity and error-handling capability, which SF2SF's simplicity doesn't fully address for complex needs. Why C fails: External Objects (Salesforce Connect) have documented limitations on relationship queries and are not a drop-in replacement for native SOQL relationship joins. Why D fails: CDC alone is a change-publishing mechanism; it requires a subscriber (middleware or Apex) to actually consume and apply changes to the other org — it isn't a complete two-org solution by itself."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "A requirement states that whenever a Case is closed, Salesforce must synchronously call three different external systems (CRM analytics, billing, and a survey tool) all within the same transaction that closes the Case. What constraint should the architect raise?", options: [("A", "There is no constraint; Apex supports unlimited synchronous callouts within a single transaction"), ("B", "Apex callouts cannot be made after a DML statement in the same transaction, and doing three synchronous callouts inline with the Case-closing DML risks violating this restriction and increases the risk of the transaction exceeding time limits"), ("C", "This is fine as long as all three calls use SOAP instead of REST"), ("D", "This is fine because governor limits only apply to standard objects like Case, not to callouts")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Apex enforces a rule that callouts cannot occur after DML within the same transaction, so a design combining the DML that closes the Case with three synchronous external callouts violates this restriction and also risks the callout timeout ceiling — requiring an async pattern like Queueable Apex or Platform Events. Why A fails: Salesforce does cap callouts per transaction (100 max) and enforces the callout-after-DML restriction, so \"unlimited\" is inaccurate. Why C fails: The DML/callout ordering restriction applies regardless of whether the callout protocol is REST or SOAP. Why D fails: Governor limits like callout restrictions apply platform-wide to Apex transactions, not selectively based on which standard object triggered them."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "A requirement asks that an Order in Salesforce and a corresponding record in an external ERP be created as a single atomic transaction — both succeed or both roll back together. What should the architect flag about this requirement given Salesforce's transaction model?", options: [("A", "Salesforce supports true two-phase commit across systems, so this is trivially achievable"), ("B", "Salesforce cannot participate in a true distributed two-phase commit with an external system; the architect must instead design a compensating transaction (saga) pattern that detects partial failure and rolls back or reconciles manually/automatically"), ("C", "This requirement is impossible to approximate in any way and should be rejected outright"), ("D", "Using Bulk API instead of REST API automatically provides two-phase commit semantics")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce Apex transactions cannot enlist in a distributed two-phase commit with an external system's database; the practical architectural response is a saga/compensating-transaction pattern that detects and corrects partial failures rather than guaranteeing atomic cross-system commits. Why A fails: No such distributed two-phase commit capability exists between Salesforce and arbitrary external systems. Why C fails: While true atomicity isn't achievable, a compensating transaction pattern is a well-established practical approximation, so outright rejection ignores available design options. Why D fails: The Bulk API is about high-volume record loading and has nothing to do with distributed transaction commit semantics."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A partner's external API enforces a strict rate limit of 10 requests per second and returns HTTP 429 errors beyond that. Salesforce needs to push updates for 50,000 records during a nightly sync. What constraint-driven design should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Send all 50,000 requests synchronously as fast as possible and rely on Salesforce retrying automatically"), ("B", "Use a queuing/throttling mechanism (e.g., middleware queue, Bulk-style batching with controlled concurrency, or a scheduled Apex/Queueable chain with delays) to stay within the partner's 10 requests/second limit and handle 429 responses with backoff"), ("C", "Ignore the rate limit since Salesforce callouts are exempt from external API throttling"), ("D", "Switch the sync to use Salesforce's internal API limits instead of the partner's limits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: When a downstream partner enforces a strict rate limit, the integration must throttle its own request rate (via queuing, controlled concurrency, or scheduled chaining) and gracefully handle 429s with backoff — this is the standard way to respect an external system's throttling policy. Why A fails: Blasting all requests at once will trigger repeated 429 rejections and Salesforce has no automatic retry/backoff behavior built in for arbitrary external throttling by default. Why C fails: External APIs' rate limits apply to any caller, including Salesforce; there is no exemption. Why D fails: Salesforce's own API limits are separate from the partner's rate limit; satisfying one does nothing to address the other."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A partner ERP undergoes a nightly maintenance window from 1–2 AM local time during which it drops all inbound connections without warning. Salesforce currently has a scheduled batch job that runs exactly at 1:30 AM to push data. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Ignore the maintenance window since Salesforce jobs always succeed regardless of target system availability"), ("B", "Reschedule the batch job outside the 1–2 AM maintenance window, and additionally implement error handling/retry logic in case the window shifts or overruns"), ("C", "Increase the batch size so the job finishes before 1 AM regardless of when it starts"), ("D", "Convert the job to real-time synchronous callouts so it can run continuously through the maintenance window")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The direct fix for a known maintenance window is to avoid scheduling conflicting work during it, while still building in resilient error handling for cases where the window shifts unexpectedly — addressing both the known constraint and its uncertainty. Why A fails: Target system unavailability during maintenance will cause connection failures regardless of Salesforce's own scheduling reliability. Why C fails: Increasing batch size doesn't guarantee the job starts and finishes entirely before 1 AM, and doesn't address the root scheduling conflict. Why D fails: Running synchronous callouts continuously would still fail during the 1–2 AM window when the ERP is dropping connections; this doesn't resolve the constraint at all."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "A new integration needs to update only the Shipping Status field on the Order object; it should not be able to delete records or access unrelated objects like Opportunity or Contact. What should the architect specify?", options: [("A", "Grant the integration user System Administrator profile for simplicity"), ("B", "Create a dedicated integration user with a custom profile/permission set granting only Edit access to the Order object (and specifically the Shipping Status field), with no delete permission and no access to unrelated objects"), ("C", "Use an existing sales rep's login credentials since they already have appropriate Order access"), ("D", "Grant Modify All Data so the integration can never be blocked by field-level security")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Principle of least privilege requires a dedicated integration user scoped with only the specific object/field/CRUD permissions actually needed — Edit on Order's Shipping Status, no delete, no access to unrelated objects. Why A fails: A System Administrator profile grants vastly more access than needed, violating least privilege and creating unnecessary security risk. Why C fails: Reusing a human user's credentials for a machine integration conflates identities, breaks audit trails, and risks disruption if that person's access changes or they leave. Why D fails: \"Modify All Data\" is an extremely broad permission that bypasses security controls entirely, the opposite of the least-privilege requirement."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "OAuth scope specification An integration only needs to read Contact records and never needs to modify data or access other API resources. What OAuth scope consideration should the architect apply when configuring the Connected App?", options: [("A", "Grant the full \"Manage user data via APIs\" (api) scope plus \"Manage user data via Web browsers\" (web) scope by default for flexibility"), ("B", "Grant only the minimum scope needed for the read-only use case (e.g., api scope restricted via a tightly scoped permission set, avoiding scopes like refresh_token or full offline access if not required) consistent with least privilege"), ("C", "Grant all available OAuth scopes so future requirements are never blocked"), ("D", "Skip OAuth scopes entirely since scopes only matter for write operations")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: OAuth scope configuration should follow least privilege — granting only what's needed for the read-only Contact use case, and avoiding broader scopes (like offline/refresh token access) unless genuinely required. Why A fails: Granting web/browser scope and full API scope by default for a narrow read-only integration is broader than necessary and increases the impact of any credential compromise. Why C fails: Granting all scopes \"for the future\" directly violates least privilege and expands the attack surface unnecessarily. Why D fails: OAuth scopes govern both read and write access; scoping matters for read-only integrations too, to prevent an over-privileged token from being used for unintended write access if compromised."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "An external partner will call Salesforce's REST API from a known, fixed set of data center IP ranges. The security team wants to ensure that even a stolen OAuth token cannot be used from an arbitrary location. What should the architect configure?", options: [("A", "Rely solely on the OAuth access token's expiration time, since IP restrictions are unnecessary once tokens expire"), ("B", "Configure IP restrictions (e.g., login IP ranges on the profile or Connected App IP relaxation settings) to allow API access only from the partner's known IP ranges, adding a network-level control beyond the token itself"), ("C", "Disable all IP restrictions to simplify partner onboarding"), ("D", "Require the partner to use a VPN, which automatically enforces IP restrictions inside Salesforce without any configuration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: IP restrictions (login IP ranges or Connected App IP relaxation policies) add a network-level control so that even a compromised token can't be used from outside the trusted IP ranges, directly addressing the stated concern. Why A fails: Token expiration limits the window of misuse but does nothing to stop a stolen token from being used from any location before it expires. Why C fails: Disabling IP restrictions removes the exact control the security team is asking for and increases risk. Why D fails: A partner using a VPN doesn't automatically configure anything inside Salesforce; IP restrictions must still be explicitly set to enforce the allowed ranges."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Bulk API vs REST vs Composite selection A nightly job must upsert 500,000 Contact records from a data warehouse into Salesforce. Which API should the architect specify?", options: [("A", "REST API with individual sObject POST calls in a loop, since REST is always simplest"), ("B", "Bulk API 2.0, since it is designed for asynchronous, high-volume batch processing and avoids the per-transaction and per-day API-call overhead of individual REST calls"), ("C", "Composite API with sub-request batching of 200 per call"), ("D", "Streaming API, since it can push all 500,000 records in one event")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Bulk API 2.0 is purpose-built for very large data volumes, processing records asynchronously in batches server-side and consuming far fewer API resources than issuing individual REST calls for 500,000 records. Why A fails: Individual REST calls for 500,000 records would consume enormous numbers of API calls and take far longer, and risks hitting daily API limits. Why C fails: Composite API sub-request batching (max 25 per composite request, or SObject Collections up to 200) is far better suited to moderate volumes, not 500,000 records. Why D fails: Streaming API/Platform Events broadcast event messages; they are not a mechanism for bulk record upsert/load operations."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "Bulk API 2.0 job lifecycle An integration developer needs to load 300,000 records and wants Salesforce to automatically handle batch-splitting without the client manually chunking the file. Which capability should the architect point to?", options: [("A", "Bulk API 1.0, which requires the client to manually split data into batches of a fixed size before submission"), ("B", "Bulk API 2.0, which accepts a single job and automatically manages internal batching/chunking on the server side, with the client only monitoring job and batch status"), ("C", "REST API SObject Collections, which auto-splits any size of data with no record limit"), ("D", "Composite Graph, which is optimized for very large flat file uploads")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Bulk API 2.0 simplifies the developer experience by accepting the whole data set as a single job and handling internal batch-splitting automatically, unlike Bulk API 1.0 which requires manual batch management. Why A fails: Bulk API 1.0 requires the client to explicitly split data into batches (up to 10,000 records each), which is the opposite of the \"automatic\" capability requested. Why C fails: SObject Collections cap at 200 records per call and do not autosplit arbitrarily large files. Why D fails: Composite Graph is designed for complex multi-object transactional requests, not large flat-file-style bulk loading."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "A trigger-based integration needs to notify an external system whenever any of up to 10,000 Opportunities are bulk-updated in a single data load, but Apex limits synchronous callouts to 100 per transaction. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Reduce the bulk update to batches of 100 records manually every time, forever, to stay under the limit"), ("B", "Move the external notification logic to an asynchronous mechanism such as Queueable Apex chaining, Batch Apex, or publishing Platform Events consumed by an external subscriber, decoupling notification volume from the sync trigger's callout limit"), ("C", "Increase the per-transaction callout limit via a support case, since Salesforce allows unlimited increases for well-justified use cases"), ("D", "Switch the trigger to before-save context, which removes all callout limits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since triggers can't safely make more than 100 synchronous callouts and can't callout in a before-save context at all reliably, the standard fix is to decouple notification into an async mechanism (Queueable, Batch Apex, or Platform Events) that can process volume without hitting the synchronous per-transaction ceiling. Why A fails: Forcing every future bulk load into manual 100-record batches is an operational burden and doesn't scale as a real architecture. Why C fails: The 100-callout limit is a hard platform governor limit and is not something Salesforce grants exceptions to via support case. Why D fails: Before-save (before insert/update) Apex contexts do not support callouts at all; this would break the integration entirely."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "Platform Events/CDC vs polling for near-real-time A logistics company wants updates on Shipment status changes reflected in an external tracking portal within 5 seconds of the change occurring in Salesforce. What should the architect recommend over REST polling?", options: [("A", "REST API polling every 5 seconds, since it guarantees sub-5-second delivery reliably at scale"), ("B", "Change Data Capture or Platform Events via the Streaming API/CometD, since they push changes to subscribers as they happen, achieving near-real-time delivery without constant polling overhead"), ("C", "A nightly Bulk API export job, since it is the most reliable delivery mechanism"), ("D", "Outbound Messaging scheduled every 5 seconds via Scheduled Apex")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: CDC/Platform Events deliver change notifications to subscribers via a persistent streaming connection as changes occur, which is the standard way to achieve near-real-time (seconds-level) delivery without the overhead and API consumption of frequent polling. Why A fails: Polling every 5 seconds is both extremely API-call-intensive and doesn't guarantee changes are caught between polls; it's not how sub-5-second reliability is typically achieved. Why C fails: A nightly export is a batch pattern with hours of latency, the opposite of the 5-second requirement. Why D fails: Outbound Messaging isn't invoked by \"scheduling\" — it fires based on workflow rule criteria on record changes, and Scheduled Apex has a minimum recurring interval far coarser than 5 seconds."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "A dealer portal needs to display live inventory counts from an external warehouse system, but the business explicitly does not want to store or replicate that data inside Salesforce for storage-cost and staleness reasons. What should the architect propose?", options: [("A", "Nightly Bulk API replication into a custom object, since it is simplest to query"), ("B", "Salesforce Connect using External Objects to virtualize the warehouse data live, querying the external system in real time without replicating it into Salesforce storage"), ("C", "Heroku Connect, since it always avoids data replication by design"), ("D", "Platform Events, storing the latest inventory count as an event payload permanently")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce Connect (External Objects) is specifically designed to virtualize external data — querying it live on demand — without persisting a replicated copy in Salesforce storage, directly matching the stated no-replication, no-staleness requirement. Why A fails: Nightly Bulk API replication both stores a copy in Salesforce (violating the no-replication requirement) and introduces staleness (up to 24 hours), the opposite of what's needed. Why C fails: Heroku Connect explicitly replicates data into a Postgres database and syncs it to Salesforce; it does not avoid replication. Why D fails: Platform Events are transient pub/sub messages, not a queryable persistent data store, and \"storing permanently\" as events misuses the feature and doesn't provide live queryability."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "Heroku Connect eventual consistency vs latency SLA A team is considering Heroku Connect to sync Salesforce Order data with a Heroku-hosted customer app, and the business requires that displayed order status must be accurate within 2 seconds of a change in Salesforce. What should the architect flag?", options: [("A", "Heroku Connect provides sub-second real-time replication by default, meeting the requirement with no configuration"), ("B", "Heroku Connect uses a polling-based sync (typically operating on intervals of seconds depending on configuration and load) with eventual consistency, so a strict 2-second guarantee may not be reliably met; a more real-time mechanism (e.g., Platform Events to the Heroku app) may be needed"), ("C", "Heroku Connect can be configured to guarantee exactly 2-second consistency by adjusting the org's API limit"), ("D", "Heroku Connect is not capable of syncing Order data under any configuration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Heroku Connect's mapping-based sync operates on a polling/trigger cadence with eventual consistency rather than a guaranteed hard real-time SLA, so a strict 2-second requirement is a risk the architect should flag and potentially address with an event-driven alternative instead. Why A fails: Heroku Connect doesn't guarantee sub-second delivery \"by default\"; it has a sync cadence and doesn't promise a specific SLA out of the box. Why C fails: There's no such Heroku Connect configuration tied to org API limits that \"guarantees\" a specific latency figure. Why D fails: Heroku Connect explicitly supports syncing standard and custom Salesforce objects including Order-like custom objects; it isn't incapable of this in general."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A field service app needs to check real-time part availability at a distributor before letting a technician commit to a repair, and the technician's workflow cannot proceed without that answer. Which pattern should be selected?", options: [("A", "Batch synchronization pattern, run hourly"), ("B", "Request-reply pattern, since the technician's next step depends on an immediate, synchronous answer from the distributor system"), ("C", "Publish-subscribe pattern, since availability changes frequently"), ("D", "Fire-and-forget pattern, since a fast response isn't critical")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Because the workflow is blocked until availability is confirmed, this is a synchronous request-reply scenario — the caller needs an immediate answer to proceed, which is the pattern's defining use case. Why A fails: Hourly batch would show stale availability data, directly undermining the requirement to check real-time availability before committing. Why C fails: Pub-sub is for decoupled, one-to-many event distribution, not a blocking single-request availability check. Why D fails: The scenario explicitly states the workflow depends on the answer, contradicting the \"fast response isn't critical\" premise of fire-and-forget."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply) A Salesforce Flow needs to call an external credit-scoring service and use the returned score to determine which approval path a loan application takes, before the Flow can continue. Which integration pattern applies?", options: [("A", "Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply), since the Flow must invoke a remote process and use its result to determine the next step before proceeding"), ("B", "Batch data synchronization, since credit scores don't change often"), ("C", "Publish-subscribe, since the credit bureau publishes scores"), ("D", "Data virtualization, since the score is external data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: This is a textbook Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply) pattern: Salesforce invokes an external process and requires its result synchronously before determining the subsequent path — exactly what's described. Why B fails: Batch synchronization would apply if scores were periodically pulled in bulk, not queried live to make an immediate routing decision. Why C fails: Pub-sub decouples publishers from subscribers with no direct request-driven interaction; here the Flow explicitly initiates a request expecting a specific reply. Why D fails: Data virtualization surfaces external data for querying as if native; it doesn't describe invoking a remote scoring computation and branching Flow logic on its result."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "A marketing team wants Salesforce to notify an external analytics platform whenever a Lead is created, but the analytics platform's processing can take up to 10 minutes and Salesforce does not need any result back. Which pattern fits, and why choose it over request-reply?", options: [("A", "Request-reply, since it's always the safer default choice"), ("B", "Fire-and-forget, since Salesforce only needs to send the notification and has no need to wait for, or act on, a response — waiting synchronously for a 10-minute process would be impractical and risk timeouts"), ("C", "Batch data synchronization, since Leads accumulate over time"), ("D", "Data virtualization, since the analytics platform holds external data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Fire-and-forget is appropriate exactly when the caller doesn't need a response and the downstream process may be long-running; waiting synchronously for a 10-minute analytics job would violate Apex callout timeout limits and serves no purpose since no reply is needed. Why A fails: Request-reply would force Salesforce to block for up to 10 minutes, exceeding the 120-second Apex callout timeout and provides no benefit since no response is actually needed. Why C fails: Batch synchronization implies periodic bulk movement, not an event-triggered one-way notification per Lead creation. Why D fails: Data virtualization is about surfacing external data for querying, unrelated to sending one-way event notifications outward."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A retailer wants to sync the previous day's completed Orders into a legacy accounting system, and the accounting system can only be safely updated between 2–4 AM when no users are active on it. Which pattern should be selected?", options: [("A", "Real-time Platform Events published on every Order completion, delivered immediately throughout the day"), ("B", "Batch data synchronization pattern, scheduled to run within the 2–4 AM window, since near-real-time isn't required and off-hours execution minimizes operational impact on the accounting system"), ("C", "Synchronous request-reply invoked from the Order completion screen"), ("D", "Data virtualization via Salesforce Connect querying the accounting system live all day")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since there's no stated need for immediacy and the accounting system has a specific safe update window, a scheduled batch sync run within that window is the pattern that both meets the business need and respects the operational constraint. Why A fails: Publishing events throughout the day would attempt to update the accounting system outside its safe window, risking disruption to active users. Why C fails: A synchronous call from the completion screen would also hit the accounting system outside the 2–4 AM window and adds unnecessary real-time coupling for a next-day batch requirement. Why D fails: Live virtualized queries all day would similarly load the accounting system outside its designated safe window and isn't needed since only completed prior-day orders matter."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "A retailer's \"Order Shipped\" event needs to be consumed by three independent systems (loyalty program, customer notification service, and returns management) that should each be able to be added, removed, or changed independently without affecting the others or Salesforce. Which pattern fits best?", options: [("A", "Three separate point-to-point Apex callouts, one hardcoded to each system"), ("B", "Publish-subscribe using Platform Events, where Salesforce publishes a single \"Order Shipped\" event and each system subscribes independently, allowing consumers to be added or removed without changing the publisher"), ("C", "A single Batch Apex job that loops through and directly updates all three external systems' databases"), ("D", "Request-reply, where Salesforce waits for acknowledgment from all three systems before proceeding")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Publish-subscribe decouples the publisher from any specific consumer; Salesforce publishes one event and any number of subscribers can independently consume it, add on, or unsubscribe without requiring changes to the publishing logic — exactly the flexibility requested. Why A fails: Hardcoded point-to-point callouts to each system tightly couple Salesforce to all three, requiring code changes whenever a consumer changes. Why C fails: Directly updating three external databases from a single job assumes direct database access (unlikely to be available or good practice) and tightly couples all three into one process. Why D fails: Waiting synchronously for acknowledgment from three independent systems this way introduces tight coupling and fragility that pub-sub is designed to avoid."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "Salesforce Connect selection over batch replication A procurement team wants Salesforce Purchase Order records to show live budget-remaining figures from an external finance system, without persisting finance data as Salesforce records, and without concern for offline access. Which is the best-fit pattern?", options: [("A", "Data virtualization via Salesforce Connect (External Objects), querying the finance system's budget data live and displaying it via SOQL/Lightning without replicating a stored copy"), ("B", "Full nightly Bulk API replication of all finance records into custom objects"), ("C", "Change Data Capture on the finance system's database, streamed into Salesforce"), ("D", "SF2SF connection to the finance system")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Salesforce Connect's data virtualization is the intended pattern for surfacing external data live inside Salesforce (queryable, displayable) without replicating a persisted copy, matching the requirement precisely, especially since offline access isn't a concern. Why B fails: Nightly full replication creates a persisted copy (violating \"without persisting\") and introduces staleness the requirement doesn't call for. Why C fails: CDC is a Salesforce feature for tracking Salesforce object changes; it isn't something you configure on an arbitrary external finance system's database. Why D fails: SF2SF is specifically for Salesforce-org-to-Salesforce-org sharing, not for connecting to a non-Salesforce external finance system."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "A company's order fulfillment process spans Salesforce, a warehouse system, and a carrier system, and the business wants a single visible control point that sequences \"reserve inventory → generate shipping label → notify carrier\" with centralized error handling and visibility into where the process currently stands. Which pattern fits best?", options: [("A", "Process choreography, since each system can react independently to any prior event with no central controller"), ("B", "Orchestration, using a central coordinating layer (e.g., MuleSoft flow or an orchestrating Apex/Flow process) that explicitly sequences the three steps and centralizes error handling/visibility"), ("C", "Fire-and-forget, since none of the three systems need to respond"), ("D", "Data virtualization, since the process spans multiple systems' data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The explicit requirement for a \"single visible control point\" that sequences steps and centralizes error handling and status visibility is the defining characteristic of orchestration, where a central coordinator drives the process end-to-end. Why A fails: Choreography intentionally has no central controller — the opposite of the stated requirement for centralized sequencing, error handling, and visibility. Why C fails: Fire-and-forget describes not waiting for responses on individual calls; it says nothing about how a multi-step sequenced process with centralized control should be structured. Why D fails: Data virtualization is about querying external data live, unrelated to sequencing a multi-step business process across systems."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "Update/Remote Call-In pattern An external warehouse management system needs to immediately push a \"Backorder Resolved\" notification into Salesforce so a case agent viewing the record sees the update without refreshing or Salesforce having to poll the warehouse system. Which pattern fits?", options: [("A", "Batch data synchronization, run every 4 hours"), ("B", "UI Update/Remote Call-In pattern, where the external system calls into Salesforce's API (e.g., REST API or Platform Events) to push the update as soon as it occurs, rather than Salesforce polling for it"), ("C", "Data virtualization via Salesforce Connect querying the warehouse system on every page load"), ("D", "Fire-and-forget from Salesforce to the warehouse system")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The Remote Call-In / UI Update pattern is specifically about an external system proactively pushing updates into Salesforce (via API call or event) as they happen, avoiding the need for Salesforce to poll — matching the \"immediately push\" requirement exactly. Why A fails: A 4-hour batch schedule directly contradicts the \"immediately\" requirement and would leave the agent viewing stale data for hours. Why C fails: Querying on every page load via Salesforce Connect adds live-query overhead on every view and still depends on the agent reloading the page rather than being proactively notified. Why D fails: Fire-and-forget in this scenario is described in the wrong direction — the trigger originates from the warehouse system pushing into Salesforce, not Salesforce calling out."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "Apex outbound callout solution An architect is designing a Salesforce outbound integration to a partner REST API, requiring secure credential storage, a defined endpoint, and use from Apex code triggered by a button click. What components should be specified?", options: [("A", "A Named Credential (storing endpoint URL and authentication details), a Remote Site Setting or Named Credential-based access (Named Credentials handle this automatically), and an Apex class using HttpRequest/Http class referencing the Named Credential, invoked from a Lightning component or Apex action tied to the button"), ("B", "Only a Remote Site Setting, since it alone is sufficient for any outbound Apex callout"), ("C", "Only Custom Metadata Types to store the endpoint URL, with credentials hardcoded in Apex"), ("D", "A Public Group and Sharing Rule, since outbound callouts are governed by record sharing settings")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A secure, maintainable outbound callout solution uses a Named Credential to centralize both the endpoint and authentication (avoiding hardcoded secrets and simplifying Remote Site Setting management), with Apex HttpRequest/Http calls referencing it, invoked from the desired UI trigger. Why B fails: A Remote Site Setting alone only allow-lists a domain for callouts; it doesn't manage authentication credentials or provide the endpoint abstraction a Named Credential does. Why C fails: Hardcoding credentials in Apex is a security anti-pattern and Custom Metadata Types alone don't solve secure credential storage or authentication handling. Why D fails: Public Groups and Sharing Rules govern record-level data access within Salesforce; they have no bearing on outbound HTTP callout configuration."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A business analyst with no Apex experience needs to build an integration that calls an external REST API to validate an address whenever a Screen Flow is used to create an Account, with minimal ongoing maintenance by a developer. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "A fully custom Apex REST callout class invoked via an Apex trigger, since only Apex can make callouts"), ("B", "External Services, which lets the REST API be registered declaratively (via its OpenAPI/Swagger schema) and then invoked as a native action inside Flow, without the analyst writing Apex"), ("C", "A Scheduled Apex batch job that runs nightly to validate addresses after the fact"), ("D", "Outbound Messaging configured on a workflow rule, since it doesn't require Apex")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: External Services is designed exactly for this: it registers an external REST API's schema declaratively and exposes it as an invocable action usable directly inside Flow, enabling low-code integration without an analyst writing Apex. Why A fails: While Apex can make callouts, the scenario explicitly calls for a solution usable by a non-Apex analyst with minimal ongoing developer maintenance, which a custom Apex class doesn't satisfy. Why C fails: A nightly batch job validates addresses after Account creation, not \"whenever\" the Screen Flow runs — this doesn't meet the real-time-in-flow requirement. Why D fails: Outbound Messaging sends a fixed SOAP message on workflow rule criteria; it's not usable as an interactive validation step inside a Screen Flow with a returned result influencing the flow."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "Platform Events component identification An architect is designing an event-driven integration where Salesforce publishes custom \"Inventory Adjusted\" events that an external Node.js service subscribes to via CometD, with the ability to resume from the last processed event after a disconnect. What components must be included?", options: [("A", "A Platform Event definition (custom fields, event bus), an event producer in Salesforce (e.g., Apex publishing the event), and a CometD-based subscriber client using the stored ReplayId to resume from the correct point after reconnecting"), ("B", "A Custom Object with a \"Notify External System\" checkbox field, polled every minute by the Node.js service"), ("C", "A Named Credential only, since it alone enables event publishing"), ("D", "A Visualforce page that streams events to the browser via JavaScript polling")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A complete Platform Events solution needs the event definition itself, a publishing mechanism (Apex or declarative), and a CometD/Streaming API subscriber that tracks and reuses the ReplayId to resume exactly where it left off after a disconnection — all three pieces are necessary. Why B fails: A checkbox field polled periodically is a polling-based workaround, not an event-driven Platform Events solution, and it can't inherently support ReplayId-based resumption. Why C fails: A Named Credential manages authentication for outbound HTTP calls; it plays no role in defining or publishing Platform Events. Why D fails: Visualforce with JS polling is a legacy client-side polling technique unrelated to CometD-based Streaming API subscription or replay capability."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "MuleSoft component roles An architect proposing a MuleSoft-based integration between Salesforce and an ERP needs to explain the roles of key components to a business stakeholder. Which correctly describes the components?", options: [("A", "Anypoint Platform is the runtime that executes Mule flows; connectors are pre-built adapters (e.g., Salesforce Connector) that simplify connecting to specific systems; the API Gateway enforces policies (rate limiting, security) on exposed APIs"), ("B", "Anypoint Platform is only a monitoring dashboard with no runtime capability; all logic must run in Salesforce Apex instead"), ("C", "Mule flows can only connect to Salesforce and cannot integrate with any other system"), ("D", "The API Gateway is a Salesforce-native feature that has nothing to do with MuleSoft")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: This accurately reflects MuleSoft's architecture: Anypoint Platform provides both design-time tooling and the Mule runtime executing integration flows, connectors are reusable adapters simplifying system-specific connections, and API Manager/Gateway enforces governance policies on exposed API endpoints. Why B fails: Anypoint Platform includes the actual Mule runtime that executes integration logic; it's far more than a monitoring dashboard, and logic doesn't have to move into Apex. Why C fails: Mule flows and connectors are designed to integrate with a very wide range of systems (ERPs, databases, SaaS apps), not just Salesforce. Why D fails: The API Gateway is a MuleSoft/Anypoint component (part of API Manager) for governing APIs, not a Salesforce-native feature."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "Salesforce Connect (OData) components An architect needs to explain the components required to surface an external product catalog inside Salesforce without replication. Which set of components correctly describes a Salesforce Connect solution?", options: [("A", "A Custom Object, a Batch Apex job, and a Scheduled Job to refresh it nightly"), ("B", "An External Data Source (configured with the appropriate adapter, e.g., OData 2.0/4.0 or a custom Apex adapter), and External Objects defined against that data source which surface the external records with SOQL-queryable fields, without persisting the data in Salesforce storage"), ("C", "A Named Credential alone, since it fully replaces the need for External Objects"), ("D", "A Platform Event definition that streams the catalog into Salesforce storage")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce Connect requires an External Data Source configured with an adapter (standard OData or custom Apex-based) and External Objects defined on top of it, which expose the external data as SOQL-queryable, non-replicated records — the correct component set for this pattern. Why A fails: A Custom Object with nightly Batch Apex refresh is a replication pattern, not Salesforce Connect's virtualization approach, and contradicts \"without replication.\" Why C fails: A Named Credential can support authentication for an External Data Source but doesn't replace the External Data Source/External Object components themselves. Why D fails: Platform Events streaming into storage both misuses events as a persistent store and directly contradicts the \"without replication\" requirement."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "SF2SF components and limitations An architect is asked to describe the native Salesforce-to-Salesforce feature's components and a key limitation for a use case requiring synchronized custom objects with complex validation rule dependencies. What should be noted?", options: [("A", "SF2SF uses Connection objects (established between two orgs) and Subscription/Publish settings per object; a key limitation is that it has constraints around syncing certain complex configurations (e.g., some custom object relationships, validation rule interplay) that may require workarounds or middleware for full fidelity"), ("B", "SF2SF requires no configuration at all and automatically syncs every object and validation rule perfectly"), ("C", "SF2SF only works between a sandbox and a production org, never between two production orgs"), ("D", "SF2SF is implemented entirely via Change Data Capture with no separate feature of its own")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: SF2SF is built on Connection objects between orgs plus per-object publish/subscribe configuration, and it has known limitations in fully replicating complex object relationships or validation-dependent behavior, which is why middleware is sometimes chosen instead for complex fidelity needs. Why B fails: SF2SF requires explicit setup (establishing connections, defining which objects/fields to publish and subscribe) — it is not automatic or zero-configuration. Why C fails: SF2SF can be configured between any two Salesforce orgs, including two production orgs, not just sandbox-to-production. Why D fails: SF2SF is a distinct native feature with its own Connection/Subscription framework; it is not simply an implementation of CDC."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "Heroku Connect components An architect needs to describe the components of a Heroku Connect-based integration for a stakeholder unfamiliar with the tool. What is the correct description?", options: [("A", "A Heroku application, a Heroku Postgres database, and the Heroku Connect add-on which manages field-level mappings between Salesforce objects and Postgres tables, keeping them synchronized on a polling cadence"), ("B", "A single Salesforce Connected App with no external database involved at all"), ("C", "A MuleSoft Anypoint runtime deployed inside Heroku's infrastructure"), ("D", "A Platform Event bus that replaces the need for any Postgres database")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Heroku Connect's architecture centers on a Heroku app backed by a Postgres database, with the Heroku Connect add-on defining object/field mappings and handling the ongoing bidirectional sync between Salesforce and Postgres on a polling cadence. Why B fails: Heroku Connect fundamentally involves an external Postgres database as the replicated data store; there's no external database-free version of this pattern. Why C fails: Heroku Connect is a distinct Heroku add-on/product, not a deployment of the MuleSoft Anypoint runtime. Why D fails: Heroku Connect relies on a Postgres database as its data store; Platform Events are a separate pub/sub mechanism and don't replace the database in this architecture."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "REST vs async Platform Events An architect is weighing a synchronous REST callout versus Platform Events for notifying a partner system of Account updates. The partner's endpoint has historically had unpredictable downtime. What trade-off should be highlighted?", options: [("A", "Synchronous REST callouts are strictly better in every scenario because they are simpler to implement"), ("B", "Synchronous REST callouts offer simplicity and immediate feedback but couple Salesforce's transaction to the partner's real-time availability (risking failures during partner downtime); Platform Events decouple the systems, letting the partner consume events whenever it recovers, at the cost of added architectural complexity and eventual (not immediate) consistency"), ("C", "Platform Events guarantee the partner will never miss an update even without implementing any replay logic"), ("D", "There is no meaningful trade-off; both approaches behave identically during partner downtime")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: This captures the real trade-off: synchronous callouts are simple but tightly couple Salesforce's success to the partner's live availability (a real risk given historical downtime), while Platform Events decouple the two systems so the partner can catch up later, at the cost of some added complexity and non-immediate consistency. Why A fails: Simplicity is only one dimension; given the partner's unpredictable downtime, \"strictly better in every scenario\" ignores the coupling risk synchronous callouts introduce. Why C fails: Platform Events only guarantee no missed updates if the subscriber correctly implements replay/ReplayId logic; this isn't automatic or guaranteed regardless of implementation. Why D fails: The behaviors are materially different during downtime — synchronous callouts fail immediately and visibly, while events queue for later consumption — so claiming no difference is incorrect."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "An architect is designing a synchronous Apex callout to a legacy mainframe service known to occasionally take up to 3 minutes to respond during peak load. What platform limit makes this design risky?", options: [("A", "The Apex callout timeout limit of 120 seconds, meaning a call that takes 3 minutes will time out before the mainframe ever responds"), ("B", "The 50,000 Platform Events per hour limit, since it applies to all synchronous callouts"), ("C", "The 15-character API name limit, since long service names cause failures"), ("D", "The 2 GB heap size limit, since heap size determines callout duration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Salesforce enforces a maximum callout timeout of 120 seconds; a mainframe service that can take up to 3 minutes (180 seconds) will exceed this and cause the callout to fail, regardless of retries, unless the design moves to an asynchronous pattern. Why B fails: The Platform Events hourly publish limit is unrelated to synchronous HTTP callout timeouts. Why C fails: API name length limits govern metadata naming conventions, not callout duration or timeout behavior. Why D fails: Heap size governs in-memory object size during Apex execution, not how long an HTTP callout is permitted to wait for a response."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "Platform Events hourly limit trade-off A high-volume retailer expects to generate up to 80,000 \"Inventory Changed\" Platform Events per hour during peak sales events, but the org's Platform Events allocation caps published events at 50,000 per hour. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Ignore the limit since Platform Events publishing is always unlimited for Enterprise Edition orgs"), ("B", "Redesign to reduce event volume (e.g., batching multiple inventory changes into a single event payload, deduplicating rapid successive changes, or filtering to only business-significant changes) to stay within the 50,000/hour limit, or evaluate a higher-capacity event allocation if available for the edition/add-on"), ("C", "Switch entirely to sending 80,000 separate outbound messages per hour instead, since Outbound Messaging has no equivalent limit"), ("D", "Simply increase the number of Salesforce licenses to raise the Platform Events limit automatically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since standard Platform Events publishing has an hourly org limit, the practical response to anticipated volume exceeding it is to reduce the effective event count through batching/deduplication/filtering, or explore an add-on/higher-tier allocation — rather than assuming the limit doesn't apply. Why A fails: Platform Events publishing limits do apply per org based on edition and any purchased add-ons; they are not unlimited by default. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging still consumes processing resources and has its own practical scaling constraints; assuming it has \"no equivalent limit\" is not a safe architectural assumption, and it doesn't solve an underlying volume problem. Why D fails: Salesforce license count (user licenses) does not directly govern the Platform Events hourly publishing allocation."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Salesforce Connect vs full replication trade-off An architect is deciding between Salesforce Connect and full data replication for a use case requiring complex roll-up summary fields and cross-object formula fields referencing the external data. What trade-off must be surfaced?", options: [("A", "Salesforce Connect fully supports roll-up summary fields and standard cross-object formulas on External Objects identically to native objects, so there is no trade-off"), ("B", "External Objects have limitations on features like roll-up summary fields and certain formula/cross-object capabilities compared to native objects, so if these declarative features are heavily required, full data replication into native custom objects may better meet the functional requirement despite the storage and staleness costs"), ("C", "Full data replication cannot support roll-up summary fields under any circumstances, making Salesforce Connect the only viable option"), ("D", "Both approaches are functionally identical in every respect, so the decision should be based solely on cost")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: External Objects (Salesforce Connect) do not support the full feature parity of native objects; roll-up summaries in particular have well-known restrictions with External Objects, so when those declarative features are essential, replicating the data into native objects (accepting storage cost and staleness) may be the better functional fit. Why A fails: External Objects do not support the full feature parity of native objects; roll-up summaries in particular have well-known restrictions with External Objects. Why C fails: Native custom objects (used in a replication approach) fully support roll-up summary fields; this option incorrectly states the opposite. Why D fails: The two approaches are not functionally identical — they differ meaningfully in supported declarative features, storage cost, and data freshness, all of which matter beyond just cost."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "REST vs SOAP API selection A legacy partner's integration team can only consume WSDL-based services with strict typed contracts and has no REST tooling in their enterprise stack. What API should the architect specify for Salesforce as the provider?", options: [("A", "REST API, since it is always the modern default choice regardless of consumer capability"), ("B", "SOAP API (Enterprise or Partner WSDL), since the partner's environment requires a strongly typed, WSDL-based contract that REST doesn't natively provide"), ("C", "Bulk API 2.0, since it is the best fit for typed contract requirements"), ("D", "GraphQL API, since it offers the most flexible schema definition")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: When a consumer's environment is built around WSDL/strongly-typed SOAP tooling and lacks REST capability, Salesforce's SOAP API (via the Enterprise or Partner WSDL) is the appropriate fit, since it natively provides the typed contract the partner's stack requires. Why A fails: \"Always modern default\" ignores the explicit stated constraint that the partner's tooling cannot consume REST. Why C fails: Bulk API is designed for high-volume asynchronous data loads, not for satisfying a typed WSDL contract requirement. Why D fails: GraphQL is about flexible querying and reducing over-fetching; it doesn't address a hard requirement for WSDL-based typed contracts."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "Bulk API 2.0 selection threshold A data migration needs to insert 15,000 new Case records in one operation. Which API is most appropriate given this volume?", options: [("A", "Single-record REST API calls, since 15,000 is a manageable volume for individual calls"), ("B", "Bulk API 2.0, since volumes in the thousands (well above the ~2,000 record threshold where Bulk API becomes advantageous) are processed more efficiently asynchronously in bulk than via many individual synchronous calls"), ("C", "Streaming API, since it can insert records directly"), ("D", "Metadata API, since it handles configuration-related bulk changes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: At volumes like 15,000 records, Bulk API 2.0's asynchronous, chunked processing is significantly more efficient and consumes far fewer API resources than issuing 15,000 individual synchronous REST calls. Why A fails: While technically possible, issuing 15,000 individual calls is inefficient, slow, and unnecessarily consumes the daily API call allocation compared to Bulk API. Why C fails: Streaming API is for event notification/subscription, not for performing record insert operations. Why D fails: Metadata API deploys configuration/metadata components (fields, objects, flows), not data records like Cases."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "Composite API for reducing API call count A mobile app needs to create an Account, a related Contact, and an Opportunity in a single logical operation, with all three either succeeding or failing together, while minimizing the number of separate API calls made. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Three separate REST API POST calls, one per object, executed sequentially"), ("B", "The Composite API (specifically Composite Graph or a composite request with allOrNone), which can create all three related records in a single API call while supporting all-or-nothing transactional behavior"), ("C", "Bulk API 2.0, since it is designed for exactly this kind of small multi-object transactional request"), ("D", "Streaming API, since events can create multiple records at once")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The Composite API (particularly Composite Graph) is designed exactly for this: performing multiple related record operations across objects in a single call, with allOrNone-style transactional rollback behavior, minimizing API call count. Why A fails: Three sequential separate calls both consume more API calls than necessary and do not provide atomic all-or-nothing behavior across the three creates. Why C fails: Bulk API 2.0 is designed for large asynchronous bulk loads of a single (or few) object types, not small transactional multi-object graphs needing an immediate synchronous result. Why D fails: Streaming API publishes/subscribes to event notifications; it has no capability to create related records transactionally."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "Platform Events vs PushTopic/Generic Streaming A team wants to define a custom event payload with fields not present on any standard or custom Salesforce object, purely for signaling a business process step. Which should the architect select?", options: [("A", "PushTopic, since it is the most flexible option for custom payloads"), ("B", "Platform Events, since they allow defining custom event objects with app-specific fields independent of any existing SObject, ideal for pure signaling use cases"), ("C", "Generic Streaming, since it supports custom field definitions natively"), ("D", "Change Data Capture, since it supports adding custom fields to change events")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Platform Events let architects define a completely custom event schema with its own fields, decoupled from any existing SObject — exactly suited to pure business-process signaling that doesn't map to standard/custom object data. Why A fails: PushTopic is SOQL-query-based, tied to changes on existing standard/custom objects, and does not support arbitrary custom field payloads independent of an object. Why C fails: Generic Streaming pushes messages via Apex but is a simpler, older mechanism intended for custom notification without SObject-based structure; Platform Events are the modern, purpose-built, and recommended choice for structured custom event definitions. Why D fails: CDC's payload structure is tied to the actual changed fields of a tracked standard/custom object; you cannot arbitrarily add unrelated custom fields to a CDC event."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "An external analytics platform wants to receive notifications whenever any of 50 different Opportunity fields change, including the specific before/after values, without Salesforce needing to write custom Apex to detect which fields changed. What should be selected?", options: [("A", "Change Data Capture, since it automatically includes changed-field information in its event payload without requiring custom Apex to compute field-level diffs"), ("B", "A custom trigger comparing Trigger.old and Trigger.new for all 50 fields, then manually building a payload"), ("C", "Outbound Messaging, since it always includes a full diff of changed fields automatically"), ("D", "Salesforce Connect, since External Objects report field-level changes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: CDC events include changed-field metadata (changedFields) automatically as part of the platform feature, meaning the specific set of fields that changed is available without the team writing custom logic to detect diffs. Why B fails: This is exactly the manual, custom-Apex approach the requirement is trying to avoid; it also doesn't scale well when many fields could change in bulk operations. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging sends a defined field payload configured on the workflow rule; it does not automatically compute or include a \"changed fields\" diff. Why D fails: Salesforce Connect (External Objects) is for surfacing external data as Salesforce records, unrelated to detecting or reporting changes to Salesforce's own Opportunity fields."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "Metadata API vs Tooling API for CI/CD A DevOps team is building a CI/CD pipeline to deploy Apex classes, custom fields, and Flow definitions between sandboxes, and also wants to run and monitor individual Apex unit tests as part of the pipeline with granular results. Which combination is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Metadata API for deploying components (classes, fields, Flows) between orgs, and Tooling API for finer-grained operations like running and monitoring individual Apex test executions"), ("B", "Bulk API for all deployment and testing operations, since it is designed for CI/CD pipelines"), ("C", "REST API SObject Collections for both deployment and test execution"), ("D", "Streaming API for deploying metadata changes in real time")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Metadata API is the standard tool for deploying metadata components (classes, fields, Flows) across orgs, while the Tooling API provides finer-grained, developer-focused operations like triggering and monitoring individual test runs — using both together fits the described CI/CD needs precisely. Why B fails: Bulk API is designed for high-volume data record operations, not metadata deployment or Apex test execution monitoring. Why C fails: SObject Collections operate on data records via REST, not metadata components or Apex test run management. Why D fails: Streaming API is for event notifications/subscriptions; it is not a metadata deployment mechanism."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "GraphQL API for reducing over-fetching A mobile app displays only three fields (Name, Phone, Email) from Contact records, but querying via standard REST composite queries returns significantly more data than needed, causing slow load times on constrained mobile networks. What should the architect propose?", options: [("A", "The GraphQL API, since it lets the client specify exactly which fields to retrieve, minimizing over-fetching and payload size for bandwidth-constrained clients"), ("B", "The Bulk API, since it is optimized for minimal payload size on mobile networks"), ("C", "SOAP API, since its XML payloads are more compact than REST/JSON"), ("D", "Metadata API, since it can restrict which fields are queryable")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The GraphQL API allows the client to declare precisely which fields it needs in the query, directly addressing over-fetching and reducing payload size — ideal for bandwidth-constrained mobile clients displaying a small subset of fields. Why B fails: Bulk API is designed for large asynchronous batch data loads, not for optimizing small, real-time mobile query payloads. Why C fails: SOAP/XML payloads are typically larger, not more compact, than equivalent JSON, and SOAP doesn't offer field-level query shaping like GraphQL. Why D fails: Metadata API manages configuration metadata, not runtime data query shaping for client applications."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "API-led connectivity layers An architect is designing a MuleSoft integration strategy and wants to explain the standard API-led connectivity layering to a stakeholder. Which correctly describes it?", options: [("A", "System layer (connects directly to backend systems like Salesforce/ERP, exposing stable APIs over unstable backends), Process layer (orchestrates and applies business logic/transformation across system APIs), Experience layer (tailors APIs for specific consuming channels/apps like mobile or web)"), ("B", "All three layers are identical and exist only for documentation purposes, with no functional difference"), ("C", "The Experience layer connects directly to backend databases, bypassing the System layer entirely"), ("D", "The Process layer is only used for logging and has no orchestration responsibility")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: This is the standard MuleSoft API-led connectivity model: System APIs abstract and stabilize access to backend systems, Process APIs compose/orchestrate business logic across system APIs, and Experience APIs shape data for specific consuming channels — a layered approach that improves reuse and insulates changes. Why B fails: Each layer has a distinct functional purpose (system abstraction, process orchestration, channel-specific shaping); they are not interchangeable or purely documentational. Why C fails: The Experience layer is designed to consume Process/System APIs, not bypass them to hit backend databases directly, which would defeat the purpose of the layered abstraction. Why D fails: The Process layer's core responsibility is orchestration and business logic composition across System APIs, not merely logging."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "OpenAPI/RAML for API-first design A team wants multiple internal developer teams to consume a new order-management API consistently, avoiding miscommunication about request/response structure, before any implementation code is written. What technique should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Writing a detailed Word document describing the API informally, updated ad hoc as implementation proceeds"), ("B", "Defining the API contract first using OpenAPI Specification (or RAML), enabling API-first design so consuming teams can review, mock, and build against a precise, machine-readable contract before implementation"), ("C", "Skipping documentation entirely and letting teams reverse-engineer the API from Apex source code"), ("D", "Using only inline code comments within the Apex REST resource class as the sole specification")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: API-first design using OpenAPI/RAML produces a precise, machine-readable, reviewable contract that multiple teams can build and mock against before implementation, directly preventing the miscommunication and rework that comes from undocumented or late documentation. Why A fails: An informal, ad hoc document lacks the precision and tooling support (mocking, validation, code generation) that a formal spec provides, and is prone to drifting out of sync. Why C fails: Reverse-engineering from source code requires implementation to exist first, directly contradicting the \"before any implementation code is written\" requirement, and increases miscommunication risk. Why D fails: Inline code comments are not accessible or reviewable by external consuming teams before implementation and don't provide a structured, tooling-friendly contract."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "An integration receives Order data in a legacy XML schema from an EDI partner and must transform it into the JSON structure Salesforce's REST API expects, including conditional logic and field concatenation. What should be used in the middleware layer?", options: [("A", "A DataWeave (or XSLT) transformation script within the middleware, capable of expressing conditional logic, field mapping, and concatenation between the source XML schema and the target JSON schema"), ("B", "A Salesforce Formula field, since formulas can transform inbound XML before it reaches Salesforce"), ("C", "A Remote Site Setting, since it defines the transformation rules for inbound data"), ("D", "A Permission Set, since it controls how fields are mapped between systems")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: DataWeave (in MuleSoft) or XSLT (in XML-centric tooling) are purpose-built data transformation languages capable of expressing conditional mapping, concatenation, and structural conversion between source and target schemas — exactly the middleware transformation task described. Why B fails: Formula fields operate on data already inside Salesforce records; they cannot transform an inbound XML payload before or as it's being received by Salesforce. Why C fails: Remote Site Settings only allow-list external domains for callouts; they have no data transformation capability. Why D fails: Permission Sets control user access/permissions within Salesforce; they have nothing to do with data format transformation logic."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "HMAC/signature verification for inbound webhooks A payment provider sends webhook notifications to a public Salesforce REST endpoint whenever a payment completes, and the security team is concerned that anyone could forge a fake \"payment completed\" call to that endpoint. What should the architect implement?", options: [("A", "Simply making the endpoint URL long and hard to guess, since obscurity alone is a sufficient security control"), ("B", "Verifying an HMAC signature (or digital signature) included in each webhook request, computed by the payment provider using a shared secret, to confirm the payload's authenticity and integrity before processing it"), ("C", "Removing authentication entirely, since webhooks are inherently trusted by design"), ("D", "Requiring only that the request come from any IP address, with no other validation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: HMAC (or digital signature) verification lets the receiving Apex REST endpoint confirm that a request genuinely originated from the payment provider and wasn't tampered with, using a shared secret to compute and compare signatures — the standard technique for securing inbound webhooks. Why A fails: \"Security through obscurity\" via an unguessable URL provides no real cryptographic assurance and can be discovered or leaked. Why C fails: Webhooks are not inherently trusted; without verification, anyone who discovers the endpoint URL could forge a malicious payload. Why D fails: IP-based checks alone are weak (IPs can be spoofed or the provider's IP range may be broad/dynamic) and don't verify payload integrity like a signature does."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "An enterprise wants centralized rate limiting, API key management, and threat protection policies applied uniformly across ten different backend APIs, including several exposed from Salesforce via External Services. Where should this enforcement point live?", options: [("A", "Individually hardcoded within each of the ten backend APIs' own codebases, duplicated ten times"), ("B", "An API Gateway (e.g., MuleSoft API Manager) sitting in front of all ten APIs, centrally enforcing rate limiting, API key validation, and threat protection policies consistently across all of them"), ("C", "Nowhere; Salesforce's org-wide daily API limits already provide all necessary rate limiting for every exposed API"), ("D", "Only within Salesforce Named Credentials, since they handle rate limiting for all outbound and inbound calls")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: An API Gateway is specifically designed as a centralized policy enforcement point — applying consistent rate limiting, key management, and security policies across many APIs from one place, avoiding duplicated and inconsistent implementations. Why A fails: Duplicating policy logic across ten codebases is exactly the inconsistency and maintenance burden an API Gateway is meant to eliminate. Why C fails: Salesforce's own daily API limits govern Salesforce-originated API consumption but don't provide governance (like API key management or threat protection) for the ten backend APIs as a set, several of which may not even be Salesforce. Why D fails: Named Credentials manage authentication for Salesforce's own outbound callouts; they are not a centralized gateway mechanism for governing multiple backend APIs' inbound traffic."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "A high-throughput order system can produce 1,000 orders per second at peak, but the downstream fulfillment system can only safely process 50 orders per second. What technique should buffer this mismatch?", options: [("A", "Message queuing (e.g., a durable queue in middleware or an MQ) placed between the two systems, absorbing bursts and feeding the fulfillment system at its sustainable processing rate"), ("B", "Increasing the fulfillment system's Salesforce API daily limit, since that governs its processing throughput"), ("C", "Removing all buffering and having the order system call the fulfillment system directly at full speed, relying on it to drop excess requests"), ("D", "Switching the order system to Bulk API, since Bulk API automatically throttles downstream systems")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A message queue is the standard technique for absorbing bursty producer throughput and feeding a slower consumer at a sustainable rate, decoupling the two systems' processing speeds without losing messages. Why B fails: Salesforce API daily limits govern Salesforce API call consumption, not the downstream fulfillment system's own processing capacity, which is an unrelated constraint. Why C fails: Calling directly at full speed with no buffering would overwhelm the fulfillment system and cause dropped or failed requests — exactly the problem queuing is meant to prevent. Why D fails: Bulk API governs how Salesforce processes bulk data jobs; it has no mechanism to throttle an unrelated downstream fulfillment system's processing rate."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "MuleSoft deployment model selection A company has strict data residency rules requiring certain customer data to never leave their own data center, but wants centralized management and monitoring of all integration flows from Anypoint Platform. What deployment model should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "CloudHub only, since it is the only Anypoint runtime option available"), ("B", "A hybrid deployment, running Mule runtime on-premise (or in a customer-managed private cloud) for flows handling the residency-restricted data, while still using Anypoint Platform's control plane for centralized management/monitoring across all flows"), ("C", "Fully on-premise with no connection to Anypoint Platform at all, since any cloud connection violates data residency"), ("D", "CloudHub exclusively, ignoring the data residency requirement since Salesforce is also cloud-based")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A hybrid deployment lets the actual data-processing runtime stay on-premise (satisfying residency requirements) while Anypoint Platform's control plane still provides centralized visibility and management across both on-prem and cloud-deployed flows — reconciling both requirements. Why A fails: CloudHub is not the only runtime option; Mule runtime can also be deployed on-premise or in a customer-managed environment, which is necessary here given the residency constraint. Why C fails: The control plane connection (for monitoring/management) is typically metadata/telemetry, not the actual restricted customer data; a fully disconnected on-prem approach isn't necessary to satisfy residency and would lose the desired centralized visibility. Why D fails: Ignoring a stated hard compliance requirement because \"Salesforce is also cloud-based\" is a non-sequitur and doesn't address the residency mandate at all."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "Salesforce as API provider: versioning strategy An architect is designing a custom Apex REST API that three different external partners will consume, and anticipates that the API's response structure will need to change over time as requirements evolve. What versioning approach should be specified?", options: [("A", "Modify the existing endpoint's response structure in place whenever changes are needed, with no versioning, since partners should always just adapt immediately"), ("B", "Include a version indicator in the URI (e.g., /services/apexrest/v1/Orders) or another versioning mechanism, so existing partner integrations on an older version continue working unaffected while a new version is introduced for evolved functionality"), ("C", "Avoid ever changing the API after its first release, permanently freezing all functionality"), ("D", "Require all three partners to redeploy their integration code simultaneously every time any change is made, coordinated manually by phone")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Explicit API versioning (e.g., in the URI path) is the standard way to let existing consumers keep working against a stable version while new capabilities are introduced under a new version, avoiding breaking changes for partners not yet ready to migrate. Why A fails: In-place breaking changes with no versioning would break all three partners' integrations without warning whenever a change is made. Why C fails: Permanently freezing the API prevents any evolution, which contradicts the stated expectation that the API will need to change over time. Why D fails: Manually coordinating simultaneous redeployment across three partners by phone is operationally fragile and unnecessary when versioning allows independent migration timing."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "An architect is building a custom Apex REST API (@RestResource) exposed to an external inventory system, expecting occasional bursts of concurrent requests. What considerations should be addressed in the design?", options: [("A", "Governor limits (e.g., SOQL query and DML limits within the Apex REST method), proper authentication/authorization enforcement (e.g., via Connected App/OAuth scopes), and appropriate HTTP status code handling for success/error responses"), ("B", "Only the URL path structure matters; governor limits do not apply to @RestResource classes"), ("C", "@RestResource classes automatically scale without any governor limit considerations, unlike other Apex code"), ("D", "Authentication is unnecessary for @RestResource endpoints since they are inherently public and safe by default")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A custom Apex REST API executes within a standard Apex transaction and is subject to the same governor limits (SOQL, DML, CPU time), so the design must account for these, plus proper OAuth-based authentication/authorization and clear HTTP status codes for consumers to handle responses correctly. Why B fails: Governor limits absolutely apply to @RestResource Apex classes just like any other Apex execution context; ignoring them risks runtime exceptions under load. Why C fails: There is no special exemption from governor limits for @RestResource classes — they run in a standard Apex transaction context subject to the same limits. Why D fails: @RestResource endpoints require appropriate authentication (typically OAuth via a Connected App); they are not inherently public or safe without configuration."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "SOAP WSDL type selection (Enterprise vs Partner) An ISV is building a single integration package to be deployed across many different customer orgs, each with different custom objects and fields, and wants the SOAP client to work generically regardless of each org's specific schema. Which WSDL should be used?", options: [("A", "Enterprise WSDL, since it is strongly typed to a specific org's exact schema at generation time, making it ideal for org-agnostic reusable packages"), ("B", "Partner WSDL, since it is loosely typed and works generically across different orgs' schemas without needing regeneration per org, making it suitable for an ISV package deployed broadly"), ("C", "Neither WSDL type works for multi-org ISV scenarios; only REST API can be used"), ("D", "Enterprise WSDL must be regenerated and hardcoded separately into the ISV package for every single customer org before it can be distributed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The Partner WSDL is loosely typed and uses generic sObject structures, allowing a single client build to work across many different orgs' schemas without regeneration — exactly suited to an ISV package meant to run generically across varied customer orgs. Why A fails: The Enterprise WSDL is strongly typed to one specific org's schema at the time it's generated, making it a poor fit for a generic package meant to run across many different orgs with different schemas. Why C fails: SOAP API (via Partner WSDL) is a valid and historically common approach for exactly this multi-org generic scenario; REST is not the only option. Why D fails: Regenerating and hardcoding the Enterprise WSDL per customer org is exactly the burden the Partner WSDL is designed to avoid, making this an impractical, non-scalable approach."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "Apex as API consumer: callout-after-DML restriction A developer writes a trigger that inserts a Task record and then, later in the same trigger execution, attempts to make an HTTP callout to notify an external system about the new Task. What will happen, and what's the fix?", options: [("A", "This works fine in all Apex execution contexts with no restriction"), ("B", "This will throw a runtime exception because Apex does not allow callouts after DML statements within the same transaction; the fix is to move the callout to an asynchronous context (e.g., a Queueable Apex enqueued from the trigger, or via Platform Events) executed in a separate transaction"), ("C", "This works fine as long as the callout uses SOAP instead of REST"), ("D", "This works fine only if the Task record is deleted immediately before the callout")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce enforces a rule that you cannot make a callout after a DML operation within the same transaction; the standard fix is to defer the callout to an asynchronous context (like Queueable Apex, which runs in its own transaction) so it executes after the DML-containing transaction has committed. Why A fails: This restriction is a real, well-documented governor limit/restriction in Apex — it is not context-independent or unconditionally permitted. Why C fails: The callout-after-DML restriction applies regardless of whether the callout protocol is REST or SOAP; protocol choice doesn't bypass it. Why D fails: Deleting the record doesn't change the fact that a DML statement (the delete itself) occurred before the callout in the same transaction; the restriction would still apply."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "External Services declarative invocation A team has an external REST API with a published OpenAPI schema and wants business analysts to invoke specific operations declaratively from within Flow, without an Apex developer writing HTTP callout code for each new operation. What Salesforce feature enables this?", options: [("A", "External Services, which registers the API's OpenAPI/Swagger schema and automatically exposes its operations as invocable actions usable directly within Flow"), ("B", "Named Credentials alone, since they alone expose callable Flow actions"), ("C", "Custom Metadata Types, since they can be configured to auto-generate Flow actions"), ("D", "Apex Interfaces, since Flow can invoke any Apex Interface without an implementing class")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: External Services reads a registered OpenAPI (or similar) schema and automatically generates invocable actions for each operation, making them directly usable inside Flow without requiring an Apex developer to hand-write callout logic for each one. Why B fails: Named Credentials manage endpoint/authentication configuration for callouts but do not themselves expose any Flow-invocable actions; they're typically used in conjunction with External Services or Apex. Why C fails: Custom Metadata Types store configuration data; they have no capability to auto-generate Flow-invocable actions from an API schema. Why D fails: An Apex Interface has no implementation on its own; Flow cannot invoke an interface without a concrete class, and this isn't how External Services actually works."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A record-triggered Flow updates an Opportunity and, in the same transaction, needs to notify three downstream systems, one of which is known to occasionally take up to 90 seconds to respond. What outbound integration option should the architect select?", options: [("A", "Three synchronous Apex callouts made directly inline within the same Flow transaction that performed the Opportunity DML"), ("B", "An asynchronous approach — such as publishing a Platform Event from the Flow (or invoking a Queueable Apex action) that is then processed in a separate transaction to make the three outbound calls, avoiding both the callout-after-DML restriction and the risk of the 90-second call impacting the triggering transaction"), ("C", "Synchronous Outbound Messaging triggered directly by the Flow's DML statement within the same transaction"), ("D", "A single Batch Apex job scheduled to run once per year")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since the Flow's transaction already contains a DML operation (the Opportunity update) and one downstream call can take up to 90 seconds (risking the callout timeout and violating the callout-after-DML rule), deferring the outbound calls to an asynchronous context via Platform Events or Queueable Apex is the correct way to decouple them safely. Why A fails: Making synchronous callouts inline after the Flow's DML would violate the callout-after-DML restriction, and the 90-second call risks exceeding practical timeout tolerances within that transaction. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging is not something you can force to fire \"synchronously within the same DML transaction\" in the way described; it is not the correct mechanism to solve this specific timing/restriction problem. Why D fails: A once-per-year scheduled job would introduce enormous delay, completely failing to meet any reasonable near-term notification need tied to an Opportunity update."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "Outbound Messaging for external subscription without polling An external analytics team wants to subscribe to changes on five custom objects without Salesforce administrators needing to configure separate workflow rules per object per field-set, and wants a single consistent event structure across all five objects. What should be recommended over Outbound Messaging?", options: [("A", "Change Data Capture, since it can be enabled per-object (up to the platform limit) and delivers a consistent event structure (header + changed fields) across all enabled objects without requiring per-field workflow rule configuration"), ("B", "Outbound Messaging, since it requires no per-object configuration at all"), ("C", "Scheduled Apex batch jobs, one per object, running every 5 minutes"), ("D", "Salesforce Connect, since it streams changes for any object without configuration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: CDC can be enabled on multiple objects (subject to org limits) and delivers a standardized event structure across all of them without needing individual workflow rules per object/field combination, exactly matching the consistency and reduced-configuration requirement. Why B fails: Outbound Messaging does require per-object, per-workflow-rule configuration (and separate field mappings), which is the opposite of the low-configuration, consistent-structure goal described. Why C fails: Five separate scheduled polling jobs reintroduce per-object configuration overhead and polling latency/inefficiency, contrary to the streamlined goal. Why D fails: Salesforce Connect is for virtualizing external data into Salesforce, not for streaming Salesforce's own object changes out to an external subscriber."),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A trigger on Contact makes a callout for every record in the Trigger.new collection, and it worked fine in testing with 5 records but throws governor limit errors when a data load updates 300 Contacts at once. What is the root cause and fix?", options: [("A", "The root cause is a network issue with the external system; the fix is to ask the external system's vendor to increase their server capacity"), ("B", "The root cause is that the trigger design doesn't account for bulk operations — making one callout per record in a large Trigger.new collection can exceed the 100-callout-per-transaction limit; the fix is to redesign for bulk-safety, e.g., batching the records into an async Queueable/Batch job that respects callout limits, or aggregating multiple records into fewer callouts"), ("C", "The root cause is a Salesforce platform bug that only Salesforce Support can fix by raising an internal limit"), ("D", "The root cause is that Contact records cannot be updated in bulk under any circumstances")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: This is the classic bulk-safety failure: code that makes one callout per record works fine for tiny record counts but breaks the 100-callout-per-transaction governor limit at scale; the standard fix is redesigning the trigger to be bulk-safe, deferring to async processing or reducing callout count via aggregation. Why A fails: The described failure is a Salesforce Apex governor limit being exceeded, not a network or external server capacity issue. Why C fails: This is a well-documented, expected governor limit behavior, not a bug, and it is not something Salesforce Support \"raises\" on request for standard per-transaction callout limits. Why D fails: Bulk Contact updates are entirely normal and well-supported in Salesforce; the issue is specifically with the trigger's non-bulk-safe callout design."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "Batchable Apex scope sizing for scale An architect is designing a Batch Apex job that calls an external API once per record, processing 2 million Account records overall, with the external API only able to reliably handle small bursts. What batch design consideration should be applied?", options: [("A", "Use the default or a small scope size (e.g., 50–200) in the Batchable's start/execute method, rather than the maximum 2,000, so each batch execution makes a manageable, throttled number of callouts consistent with the external API's burst tolerance"), ("B", "Always set scope size to the maximum of 2,000 regardless of external system capacity, since larger batches always perform better"), ("C", "Batch Apex cannot make callouts, so this approach is not possible at all"), ("D", "Use a single batch with no scope limit to process all 2 million records in one execution")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Batchable Apex lets you specify a scope size controlling how many records are processed per execute() invocation; choosing a smaller size appropriate to the external API's burst tolerance prevents overwhelming a fragile downstream system while still processing the full 2 million records across many batches. Why B fails: Maximizing scope size ignores the stated external system constraint and risks overwhelming an API that can only handle small bursts, causing failures. Why C fails: Batch Apex execute() methods can make callouts (when the class implements Database.AllowsCallouts), so this is a valid and common pattern. Why D fails: There's no single-batch-with-no-limit mode; Batch Apex inherently divides work into scoped batches, and attempting to remove scoping isn't how the framework works nor advisable at this volume."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "Platform Event delivery guarantees and idempotent consumers An external subscriber occasionally receives the same Platform Event twice due to at-least-once delivery semantics. What must the subscriber's design account for?", options: [("A", "Nothing; Platform Events guarantee exactly-once delivery so duplicates are impossible"), ("B", "The subscriber must be designed to be idempotent — e.g., using a unique event identifier or business key to detect and safely ignore or safely reprocess duplicate events without causing incorrect duplicate side effects"), ("C", "The subscriber should simply process every event exactly as received, since duplicate processing has no real-world consequences"), ("D", "The publisher, not the subscriber, is solely responsible for guaranteeing no duplicates are ever sent")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Platform Events use at-least-once delivery, meaning duplicates are possible; subscribers must be designed idempotently (e.g., checking a unique event ID or business key before applying side effects) to avoid incorrect results from reprocessing the same event. Why A fails: Platform Events explicitly provide at-least-once, not exactly-once, delivery — duplicates are an expected possibility that must be handled. Why C fails: Assuming duplicate processing \"has no consequences\" is unsafe in general — e.g., duplicate order creation or duplicate financial postings would cause real business problems. Why D fails: Given the at-least-once delivery model, the platform does not guarantee the publisher can prevent all duplicates; responsibility for correctness under duplicates falls to the subscriber's idempotent design."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "ReplayId for subscriber recovery A CometD-based subscriber to a Platform Event goes offline for 2 hours due to a server crash, and events published during that window must not be lost when the subscriber comes back online. What mechanism should be used?", options: [("A", "The subscriber should reconnect and use a stored ReplayId (or -2 for all retained events) to resume streaming from the correct point, replaying any events published during the outage, within the platform's event retention window"), ("B", "Nothing can be done; any events published while the subscriber was offline are permanently and unavoidably lost"), ("C", "The publisher must manually resend every event that occurred during the outage window via a new Apex script"), ("D", "The subscriber should simply reconnect and only receive brand-new events going forward, ignoring the gap entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The Streaming API's replay mechanism lets a reconnecting subscriber supply a ReplayId (from its last successfully processed event, or -2 to replay all retained events) to resume exactly where it left off, recovering missed events within the platform's retention window. Why B fails: This ignores the entire purpose of the ReplayId/replay mechanism, which exists specifically to prevent this kind of data loss during subscriber outages. Why C fails: Manually resending events via a new script duplicates functionality the platform already provides natively through ReplayId-based resumption, and risks introducing its own errors/duplicates. Why D fails: Ignoring the gap defeats the purpose of reliable event processing and would silently lose two hours of business-relevant events."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "MuleSoft A MuleSoft integration is experiencing throughput bottlenecks during peak load, processing messages too slowly to keep up with incoming volume from Salesforce. What scaling technique should the architect apply within Anypoint/CloudHub?", options: [("A", "Reduce the number of Mule workers to lower cost, which will proportionally increase throughput"), ("B", "Scale horizontally by increasing the number of Mule workers (or enabling clustering/vCore scaling in CloudHub) so incoming load is distributed across multiple concurrent runtime instances, increasing overall throughput"), ("C", "Switch the flow to process messages strictly one at a time with no concurrency, since serial processing is always faster"), ("D", "Move the flow's logic entirely into a single Salesforce Apex trigger to bypass MuleSoft scaling limits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Horizontal scaling — adding more Mule workers or enabling clustering/increased vCore allocation in CloudHub — lets the integration distribute processing load across multiple concurrent runtime instances, directly addressing a throughput bottleneck under peak load. Why A fails: Reducing worker count would decrease available processing capacity, worsening the throughput bottleneck rather than improving it. Why C fails: Serial, non-concurrent processing generally reduces throughput compared to appropriately parallelized processing, especially under high load — this is the opposite of a scaling fix. Why D fails: Moving the entire integration logic into a Salesforce Apex trigger introduces Apex's own governor limits and doesn't solve a MuleSoft throughput scaling problem; it essentially abandons the middleware architecture."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "Salesforce Connect custom Apex adapter for non-standard sources A team needs to virtualize data from a proprietary internal system that has no OData interface and no standard adapter available. What Salesforce Connect approach should be used?", options: [("A", "A custom Apex-based Salesforce Connect adapter (implementing the Connect Framework's Apex classes) to translate calls to/from the proprietary system's native interface, exposed as External Objects"), ("B", "The standard OData 4.0 adapter, since it works with any system regardless of whether it exposes OData"), ("C", "Heroku Connect, since it supports any arbitrary proprietary system format automatically"), ("D", "This scenario cannot be supported by Salesforce Connect under any circumstances")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: When no standard OData interface exists, Salesforce Connect supports building a custom Apex adapter (implementing the appropriate Connect Framework interfaces) to bridge to the proprietary system's native protocol, still exposing the result as queryable External Objects. Why B fails: The standard OData adapter specifically requires the source system to expose an OData-compliant interface; it cannot connect to a proprietary system lacking one. Why C fails: Heroku Connect is specifically built around Postgres-based data synchronization, not a generic connector for any arbitrary proprietary system protocol. Why D fails: This is incorrect — the custom Apex adapter capability exists precisely to support non-standard/proprietary sources that lack a standard adapter."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "Heroku Connect mapping polling interval vs API consumption trade-off An architect configuring Heroku Connect mappings must balance how quickly changes sync against Salesforce API consumption. What should be considered?", options: [("A", "Polling interval and field selection are irrelevant since Heroku Connect never consumes Salesforce API calls"), ("B", "More frequent polling intervals and syncing more fields/objects increase how current the replicated data is, but also increase Salesforce API call consumption; the design should balance the business's freshness requirements against the org's available API allocation"), ("C", "Heroku Connect should always be configured to poll as frequently as technically possible, regardless of the business's actual freshness requirements or API budget"), ("D", "Field selection has no effect on API consumption, only polling interval does")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Heroku Connect's sync mappings consume Salesforce API calls as they poll for changes; more frequent polling and larger field/object scope increase data freshness but also increase API consumption, so the architect must balance these against the org's actual freshness needs and available API budget. Why A fails: Heroku Connect does consume Salesforce API calls as part of its polling-based synchronization mechanism; it is not API-call-free. Why C fails: Maximizing polling frequency without regard to actual business need or API budget risks unnecessarily exhausting the org's daily API allocation for no real benefit. Why D fails: Broader field/object selection in a mapping also affects the volume/frequency of data pulled and thus API consumption, not just the polling interval alone."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "An integration occasionally fails with a network timeout connecting to a partner API, and separately sometimes fails because the partner rejects a payload for missing a required tax ID field. How should these two failure types be classified?", options: [("A", "Both are the same type of error and should be handled identically with a simple retry"), ("B", "The timeout is a system error (transient, infrastructure/connectivity-related, often retry-appropriate); the missing-field rejection is an application error (a business/validation failure that a blind retry won't fix, since the payload itself is invalid)"), ("C", "Both are application errors, since they both occur within an \"application\" context"), ("D", "The timeout is an application error and the missing field is a system error")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: System errors (like network timeouts) stem from infrastructure/connectivity issues and are often transient, making retries appropriate; application errors (like a validation rejection) stem from a problem with the data/business logic itself, where retrying the identical payload will simply fail again. Why A fails: Treating both identically with a blind retry would cause the missing-field case to fail repeatedly without ever fixing the underlying data problem. Why C fails: A network timeout is fundamentally a connectivity/infrastructure issue, not a business validation problem, so classifying it as an application error mischaracterizes its root cause. Why D fails: This reverses the correct classification — a timeout is infrastructure-related (system), and a missing required field is a data/business validation issue (application)."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "Given the distinction between system and application errors, what handling strategy should the architect implement for each type in an order-submission integration?", options: [("A", "For both types, silently discard the failed record with no logging or notification"), ("B", "For system errors, implement automatic retry with backoff (and eventual escalation if retries exhaust); for application errors, capture the specific validation failure and route it to a business user/queue for correction rather than blindly retrying the same invalid data"), ("C", "For system errors, immediately notify a human to manually resubmit each request one at a time; for application errors, automatically retry indefinitely"), ("D", "Treat every failure, regardless of type, as unrecoverable and abandon the entire integration permanently")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: This matches each error type to an appropriate strategy: system errors benefit from automated retry/backoff since they're often transient, while application errors need human/business correction of the underlying data since retrying an invalid payload as-is won't succeed. Why A fails: Silently discarding failures with no logging or notification hides problems from the business and prevents any correction or root-cause analysis. Why C fails: This reverses the appropriate strategies — manual intervention makes more sense for application/validation errors, while automated retry fits transient system errors, not vice versa; indefinite retry of bad data will never succeed. Why D fails: Abandoning the entire integration for any single failure is a drastic overreaction that ignores the fact that most integration failures are per-record or per-transaction, not systemic."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "An Apex callout to a partner API occasionally fails due to the partner's service being briefly overloaded, and immediately retrying at fixed short intervals seems to make the problem worse by adding more load. What retry strategy should be applied?", options: [("A", "Retry immediately and repeatedly with no delay between attempts, as fast as possible, to resolve the issue before the user notices"), ("B", "Exponential backoff — retrying with progressively longer delays between attempts (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s) — reduces load on the already-struggling partner service and increases the likelihood that the retry succeeds once the partner recovers"), ("C", "Retry exactly once immediately and never attempt again regardless of outcome"), ("D", "Increase the number of concurrent retry threads to overwhelm the partner service until it responds")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Exponential backoff spaces out retry attempts with increasing delays, which reduces additional load on an already struggling service and gives it time to recover, improving the odds of eventual success compared to rapid-fire retries. Why A fails: Rapid, undelayed retries add more load to an already overloaded service, worsening the exact problem described in the scenario. Why C fails: A single immediate retry with no further attempts may fail again if the partner hasn't yet recovered, missing the chance for eventual success once load subsides. Why D fails: Increasing concurrent retry threads adds more simultaneous load on a struggling service, the opposite of what's needed to help it recover."),
        Question(id: "101", question: "IDs A batch integration occasionally fails partway through and must be safely rerun from the beginning without creating duplicate Account records for rows that were already successfully processed. What technique should the architect apply?", options: [("A", "Always use a plain Insert operation on retry, accepting that some duplicates may occur as an unavoidable cost"), ("B", "Use Upsert operations keyed on a unique External ID field (populated from the source system's unique identifier), so rerunning the batch updates already-created records instead of creating duplicates"), ("C", "Manually query and delete all Account records before every rerun, then reinsert everything from scratch"), ("D", "Disable all validation rules during reruns so duplicate records can be created and cleaned up later")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Upserting on a unique External ID makes the batch operation idempotent — rerunning it identifies already-created records by their source system key and updates them instead of creating duplicates, safely supporting reruns after partial failure. Why A fails: Plain Insert has no way to recognize previously created records, so rerunning after a partial failure will create duplicate Accounts for rows already processed. Why C fails: Deleting and reinserting everything on every rerun is destructive, risky (loses any interim manual edits), and unnecessarily heavy-handed compared to a targeted upsert. Why D fails: Disabling validation rules doesn't address duplicate detection at all and introduces unrelated risk by bypassing data integrity checks."),
        Question(id: "102", question: "Outbound Messaging acknowledgment handling An architect configures Salesforce Outbound Messaging to notify a legacy system on Case updates, but that legacy endpoint is known to be unreliable and doesn't always respond quickly. What should the architect explain about Salesforce's built-in behavior here?", options: [("A", "Salesforce Outbound Messaging has no retry mechanism at all; if the first attempt fails, the message is permanently lost"), ("B", "Salesforce automatically retries delivering an unacknowledged outbound message, continuing retry attempts for a period of time (up to 24 hours) before giving up, providing some built-in resilience against transient endpoint issues"), ("C", "Salesforce Outbound Messaging requires the architect to manually write custom Apex retry logic, since there's no built-in retry"), ("D", "Salesforce guarantees an outbound message will always be delivered eventually with no time limit whatsoever")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce's Outbound Messaging includes built-in retry behavior for unacknowledged messages, continuing attempts for up to 24 hours before giving up — a meaningful degree of built-in resilience the architect can rely on without custom retry code. Why A fails: Outbound Messaging does have built-in retry logic for unacknowledged messages; it is not a single-attempt, no-retry mechanism. Why C fails: The retry behavior is native/built-in to the Outbound Messaging feature; the architect does not need to write custom Apex to get basic retry behavior. Why D fails: The retry window is bounded (up to 24 hours), not indefinite; after that period, Salesforce stops retrying if the endpoint remains unresponsive."),
        Question(id: "103", question: "Bulk API partial success handling A data load job for 10,000 records is configured with allOrNone=false, and 200 records fail validation while 9,800 succeed. What should the architect design for handling this outcome?", options: [("A", "Since allOrNone=false was used, the entire job automatically rolls back if even one record fails, so no special handling is needed"), ("B", "Capture and review the per-record error results for the 200 failed records (available in the job's result files), correct or route them for handling (e.g., a re-load after fixing source data), while the 9,800 successful records remain committed"), ("C", "Discard all 10,000 records, including the 9,800 successes, since partial success is not supported by Bulk API"), ("D", "Ignore the 200 failures permanently since Bulk API does not provide any way to identify which specific records failed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: With allOrNone=false, Bulk API commits successful records independently of failed ones, and provides per-record result/error details so the architect can design a process to review, correct, and reprocess just the failed subset without touching the already-committed successes. Why A fails: allOrNone=false specifically means partial success is allowed — the successful records are NOT rolled back just because some records failed. Why C fails: Bulk API explicitly supports partial success with allOrNone=false; discarding the whole batch would contradict the very setting used. Why D fails: Bulk API result files do report per-record success/failure status and error messages, making it possible to identify exactly which records failed and why."),
        Question(id: "104", question: "MuleSoft error handler routing (on-error-continue vs on-error-propagate) A MuleSoft flow processes a batch of Salesforce records; for one record type of error, the flow should log it and continue processing remaining records, while for another critical error type, the entire flow should stop and propagate the failure to alert operations immediately. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "Use on-error-continue for the error type where processing should continue after logging, and on-error-propagate for the critical error type that should halt the flow and surface the failure upstream for alerting"), ("B", "Use on-error-propagate for every error type universally, since it is always the safer choice"), ("C", "Use on-error-continue for every error type universally, since it always keeps the flow running regardless of severity"), ("D", "Error handling scopes in Mule flows cannot differentiate behavior by error type")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Mule's error handling framework allows different error types to be routed to different handlers — on-error-continue lets the flow recover and proceed after logging a non-critical error, while on-error-propagate deliberately stops the flow and surfaces critical failures for alerting, matching the two described needs exactly. Why B fails: Using on-error-propagate universally would halt the flow even for the non-critical error type that should simply be logged and continued past. Why C fails: Using on-error-continue universally would suppress the critical error's propagation, defeating the requirement for it to halt and alert operations immediately. Why D fails: Mule flows explicitly support type-specific error handling (e.g., matching on error type/namespace) precisely so different handlers can apply to different error conditions."),
        Question(id: "105", question: "Platform Event subscriber occasionally fails to process a specific event due to a malformed payload, and the team wants failed events captured for later investigation without blocking the processing of subsequent valid events. What pattern should be applied?", options: [("A", "Halt the entire subscriber permanently the first time any event fails to process"), ("B", "A dead-letter queue pattern — routing the failed event (with error context) to a separate queue/store for later investigation and reprocessing, while the subscriber continues processing subsequent events normally"), ("C", "Silently drop the failed event with no record of it, and continue processing"), ("D", "Reprocess the same malformed event in an infinite retry loop before allowing any further events to be processed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A dead-letter queue captures failed messages/events (with enough context to investigate) in a separate location so the main processing pipeline can continue handling subsequent events without getting stuck or losing visibility into the failure. Why A fails: Halting the entire subscriber permanently over a single malformed event would block all subsequent legitimate events, causing unnecessary business impact. Why C fails: Silently dropping the event with no record prevents any investigation or reprocessing, risking undetected data loss. Why D fails: An infinite retry loop on an unprocessable malformed payload will never succeed and will block the subscriber from making progress on later events."),
        Question(id: "106", question: "An order process creates a Salesforce Order record, then calls an external payment system, then calls an external shipping system — and the payment call succeeds but the shipping call fails. Since there's no distributed two-phase commit, what should the architect design?", options: [("A", "Leave the Order and successful payment in place with no further action, ignoring the failed shipping step entirely"), ("B", "A compensating transaction (saga pattern step) that, upon detecting the shipping failure, either retries shipping separately or triggers a compensating action (e.g., reversing/refunding the payment or flagging the Order for manual reconciliation), rather than assuming atomicity across the three systems"), ("C", "Roll back the Salesforce Order and the external payment automatically via a native cross-system two-phase commit"), ("D", "Prevent this scenario by making all three systems synchronous, which automatically guarantees atomicity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: In the absence of true cross-system atomicity, the saga pattern responds to partial failure with an explicit compensating action (like a refund or reconciliation flag) so the overall business state is corrected/handled deliberately rather than left inconsistent. Why A fails: Ignoring the failure leaves an inconsistent state — a paid order with no shipment — which harms the customer experience and financial accuracy. Why C fails: No native two-phase commit exists across Salesforce and two external systems; assuming automatic rollback across all three is not technically achievable this way. Why D fails: Making calls synchronous does not itself provide distributed atomicity; a synchronous chain can still partially fail after an earlier step (payment) has already succeeded."),
        Question(id: "107", question: "OAuth Web Server flow for user-context inbound integration A third-party mobile app needs to access and act on Salesforce data as the specific logged-in Salesforce user, with the user explicitly consenting via a login/consent screen. Which OAuth flow fits?", options: [("A", "OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow, since it involves a browser-based redirect where the user authenticates and explicitly grants consent, issuing tokens scoped to that specific user's context"), ("B", "OAuth 2.0 JWT Bearer flow, since it is designed for interactive user consent screens"), ("C", "OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials flow, since it operates in the context of the specific logged-in user"), ("D", "SAML Bearer flow, since it requires no user interaction whatsoever")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The Web Server flow is specifically designed for scenarios where a user interactively logs in and grants consent via a redirect-based browser flow, resulting in tokens tied to that user's own data access context — precisely what's described. Why B fails: JWT Bearer flow is designed for server-to-server authentication without any interactive user consent screen, the opposite of what's needed here. Why C fails: Client Credentials flow authenticates as a service account/application identity, not as a specific interactively-consenting end user. Why D fails: SAML Bearer flow is used for federated assertion-based authentication, typically without an interactive Salesforce login/consent screen, which doesn't match the explicit user consent requirement."),
        Question(id: "108", question: "Named Credential per-user authentication A company wants an outbound integration where each Salesforce user's calls to an external system are made using that specific user's own external system credentials/permissions, rather than one shared service account for everyone. What Named Credential configuration should be used?", options: [("A", "A single org-wide Named Credential shared by all users with one fixed service account"), ("B", "A Named Credential configured for per-user (user-specific) authentication, where each user authenticates individually and Salesforce stores/uses each user's own external credentials/tokens for their callouts"), ("C", "Hardcoded credentials embedded directly in Apex, identical for every user"), ("D", "A Named Credential with no authentication configured at all, relying on IP allowlisting only")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Named Credentials support a per-user (user-specific) authentication mode where each user goes through their own authentication to the external system, and their individual credentials/tokens are used for their own callouts — directly matching the requirement for per-user external access rather than one shared identity. Why A fails: An org-wide Named Credential with one shared account would make every user's calls appear identical to the external system, the opposite of maintaining per-user identity/permissions externally. Why C fails: Hardcoded credentials shared across all users in Apex would similarly erase per-user identity and is a security anti-pattern regardless. Why D fails: IP allowlisting alone provides no user-specific authentication at all; it only restricts by network origin, not by individual user identity."),
        Question(id: "109", question: "Connected App admin-approved users policy A Connected App integrates with a sensitive external finance system, and the security team wants to ensure that only specifically pre-authorized users (not just anyone in the org) can use this integration, even though many users technically have API access. What Connected App setting should be configured?", options: [("A", "Set the OAuth policy to \"All users may self-authorize,\" since this is the most secure setting"), ("B", "Set the OAuth policy to \"Admin approved users are pre-authorized,\" and then explicitly assign only the intended users/profiles/permission sets to the Connected App"), ("C", "Disable the Connected App entirely, since Connected Apps cannot restrict which users can use them"), ("D", "Grant \"Modify All Data\" to every user so the Connected App works consistently for everyone")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The \"Admin approved users are pre-authorized\" policy requires an administrator to explicitly assign specific profiles or permission sets to the Connected App, ensuring only pre-authorized individuals can use it rather than any user with general API access. Why A fails: \"All users may self-authorize\" allows any user to grant themselves access without admin pre-approval, the opposite of the tightly controlled access the security team wants. Why C fails: Connected Apps explicitly support restricting usage to pre-approved users via this OAuth policy setting; disabling it entirely isn't necessary and removes the integration's functionality altogether. Why D fails: \"Modify All Data\" is an unrelated, overly broad permission that has nothing to do with which users are authorized to use a specific Connected App, and it violates least privilege."),
        Question(id: "110", question: "Connected App IP relaxation policy A partner integration calls Salesforce APIs using OAuth tokens from a cloud provider's dynamic IP range that changes frequently and cannot be fully enumerated in advance, but the org enforces login IP restrictions on the associated profile. What Connected App setting addresses this without removing IP security entirely elsewhere?", options: [("A", "Remove all login IP range restrictions from the profile entirely, applying no IP-based control anywhere"), ("B", "Configure the Connected App's IP Relaxation setting to relax IP restrictions specifically for OAuth tokens issued through that Connected App, while leaving other login IP restrictions (e.g., direct username/password login) intact elsewhere"), ("C", "Set the Connected App to require a VPN, which automatically bypasses IP restriction enforcement"), ("D", "There is no way to accommodate a dynamic IP range in Salesforce; the partner must switch to a static IP")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The Connected App's IP Relaxation setting lets you selectively relax IP enforcement for OAuth tokens issued through that specific Connected App, without having to remove IP restrictions globally from the profile for other login paths. Why A fails: Removing all IP restrictions everywhere is a much broader security reduction than needed; the Connected App-specific relaxation setting exists precisely to avoid this blunt approach. Why C fails: There's no Connected App \"require VPN\" setting that automatically bypasses IP enforcement; IP Relaxation is the actual mechanism for this scenario. Why D fails: IP Relaxation at the Connected App level is exactly the accommodation mechanism for dynamic/unenumerable partner IP ranges; a static IP requirement isn't the only solution."),
        Question(id: "111", question: "Connected App refresh token policy A security team wants OAuth refresh tokens issued to a particular Connected App to expire automatically if the associated user hasn't used the integration in 90 days, reducing the risk of dormant, potentially compromised tokens being used later. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "A refresh token policy on the Connected App set to expire the refresh token after a period of inactivity (e.g., \"expire refresh token if not used for X days,\" configured to the desired window)"), ("B", "Refresh tokens in Salesforce never expire under any configuration and this cannot be controlled"), ("C", "Disable OAuth entirely for the Connected App, since that is the only way to expire tokens"), ("D", "Set the Connected App's session timeout value to 90 minutes, which controls refresh token expiration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Salesforce Connected Apps support a refresh token policy that can expire the token after a configured period of inactivity, directly addressing the risk of dormant, unused tokens remaining valid indefinitely. Why B fails: Refresh token expiration policies are a configurable Connected App setting; \"never expire under any configuration\" is factually incorrect. Why C fails: Disabling OAuth entirely removes the integration's functionality altogether rather than selectively managing token lifetime, which isn't necessary given the available policy setting. Why D fails: Session timeout settings govern user session length in the UI context, not the OAuth refresh token's own expiration-on-inactivity policy."),
        Question(id: "112", question: "Salesforce as SAML IdP vs SP A company wants users who log into Salesforce to be automatically signed into a separate internal expense reporting tool without re-entering credentials, using Salesforce as the source of the authenticated session. What SAML role should Salesforce play?", options: [("A", "Salesforce should be configured as the SAML Service Provider (SP), since Salesforce always initiates authentication"), ("B", "Salesforce should be configured as the SAML Identity Provider (IdP), issuing a SAML assertion after the user logs into Salesforce, which the expense tool (acting as the Service Provider) consumes to establish a session without requiring the user to log in again"), ("C", "SAML cannot be used in this direction; only OAuth can enable this kind of delegated login"), ("D", "Salesforce must always be the SAML SP any time it interacts with another SAML-enabled system")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since Salesforce is the system where the user first authenticates and the expense tool needs to trust that established identity, Salesforce plays the Identity Provider (IdP) role — issuing the SAML assertion — while the expense tool acts as the Service Provider (SP) consuming it, enabling single sign-on without re-authentication. Why A fails: Salesforce is not the SP in this scenario; it is the source of the authenticated identity being asserted outward, which is the IdP role, not the relying/consuming SP role. Why C fails: SAML delegated/federated login (IdP-initiated SSO) is exactly designed for this cross-application single sign-on scenario; it isn't limited only to OAuth. Why D fails: Salesforce's SAML role (IdP or SP) depends on the specific integration direction; Salesforce can and does act as an IdP for other applications in exactly this kind of scenario, not always as the SP."),
        Question(id: "113", question: "A financial partner requires that both the client and server present and validate X.509 certificates before any data is exchanged, rejecting connections that only authenticate the server. What must the architect configure in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "A standard one-way TLS connection where only the partner's server certificate is validated, since this fully satisfies the mutual authentication requirement"), ("B", "Mutual TLS (mTLS): upload/configure a client certificate in Salesforce's certificate keystore, associate it with the outbound Named Credential/HTTP call, so Salesforce presents its own client certificate while also validating the partner's server certificate"), ("C", "Basic authentication with a username and password, which satisfies certificate-based mutual authentication requirements"), ("D", "IP allowlisting alone, since it is functionally equivalent to mutual certificate-based authentication")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Mutual TLS requires both parties to present certificates; Salesforce supports this by storing a client certificate in its certificate keystore and associating it with the outbound callout (via Named Credential configuration), so Salesforce authenticates itself to the partner via certificate while also validating the partner's server certificate. Why A fails: One-way TLS only validates the server's certificate; it explicitly does not satisfy a mutual (two-way) certificate authentication requirement, which the partner is mandating. Why C fails: Username/password Basic auth is a completely different authentication mechanism and does not involve X.509 certificate-based mutual authentication at all. Why D fails: IP allowlisting is a network-level control unrelated to cryptographic certificate-based mutual authentication; it is not functionally equivalent."),
        Question(id: "114", question: "Shield Platform Encryption impact on integrations A field on Contact is encrypted using Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption (probabilistic/deterministic encryption). An outbound Bulk API integration needs to extract and use this field's values in an external system, and a separate SOQL query needs to filter on this field using a WHERE clause. What should the architect flag?", options: [("A", "Shield Platform Encryption has zero impact on SOQL queries, Bulk API, or outbound data visibility for encrypted fields"), ("B", "Filtering (WHERE clause) capability on Shield-encrypted fields is limited depending on the encryption scheme (e.g., deterministic encryption supports exact-match filtering while probabilistic does not support filtering at all), and Bulk API/outbound extracts will return the field's ciphertext or decrypted value depending on the running user's \"View Encrypted Data\" permission — both must be accounted for in the integration design"), ("C", "Shield Platform Encryption automatically decrypts all fields for any integration user regardless of permissions"), ("D", "Shield Platform Encryption prevents all API access to encrypted fields under every circumstance, making this integration impossible")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Shield Platform Encryption has real, documented implications: SOQL filtering capability depends on the encryption scheme used (deterministic supports certain exact-match operations, probabilistic does not support filtering), and whether an API caller sees plaintext or ciphertext depends on whether the running user holds the \"View Encrypted Data\" permission — both are critical design considerations for this integration. Why A fails: Shield Platform Encryption absolutely does affect SOQL filtering capability and what value (encrypted or decrypted) is returned via API/Bulk extracts; \"zero impact\" is inaccurate. Why C fails: Decryption via API is explicitly gated by the \"View Encrypted Data\" permission; it is not automatic for every integration user regardless of permission assignment. Why D fails: Encrypted fields remain accessible via API for authorized users/integrations (subject to permission and encryption-scheme constraints); it does not make all API access categorically impossible."),
        Question(id: "115", question: "OAuth Device Flow for headless/IoT A warehouse IoT scanner device has no browser and very limited input capability (just a numeric keypad), but needs to authenticate to Salesforce to submit scan data, with a human warehouse worker completing an approval step on their own phone/browser. Which OAuth flow fits?", options: [("A", "OAuth 2.0 Device Flow, where the device displays or communicates a short code, the user visits a URL on a separate browser-capable device to enter the code and approve access, and the device then polls for and receives its token — designed exactly for browser-less/limited-input devices"), ("B", "OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow, since the device itself must perform a full browser redirect"), ("C", "SAML Bearer flow, since it eliminates the need for any device-side interaction"), ("D", "Username-password OAuth flow, since it is the most modern and recommended approach for IoT devices")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The Device Flow is specifically designed for input-constrained, browser-less devices: the device obtains a short code, a human completes the approval on a separate capable device/browser, and the constrained device polls until the token is issued — exactly matching this IoT scanner scenario. Why B fails: The Web Server flow requires the authenticating client itself to perform a browser redirect, which a browser-less device with only a numeric keypad cannot do. Why C fails: SAML Bearer flow involves passing a SAML assertion for authentication, not a code-based device/human split; it doesn't match this specific browser-less approval-by-proxy scenario. Why D fails: The username-password OAuth flow (Resource Owner Password Credentials) is a legacy, discouraged flow due to security concerns (credentials handled by the client), and is not the modern recommended approach for constrained IoT devices — Device Flow is."),
        Question(id: "116", question: "Salesforce deprecations An integration hardcodes API version 40.0 in its endpoint URLs, and the architect learns Salesforce periodically retires very old API versions. What resilience practice should be adopted?", options: [("A", "Leave the version hardcoded permanently at 40.0 forever, since Salesforce never actually retires old API versions in practice"), ("B", "Establish a practice of periodically reviewing and updating the API version used by the integration (staying within Salesforce's supported version window) as part of ongoing maintenance, rather than leaving it frozen indefinitely on an aging version"), ("C", "Switch to not specifying any API version at all in any request, since omitting a version guarantees permanent forward compatibility"), ("D", "Rewrite the entire integration in a completely different API (e.g., swap in Bulk API for what was a REST API integration) purely to avoid ever specifying a version number")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since Salesforce does periodically retire old API versions, a resilient integration should include a practice of periodically reviewing and bumping the API version used, staying within the supported window, rather than leaving it frozen indefinitely and risking eventual breakage. Why A fails: Salesforce does retire old API versions over time (per its published version support policy), so assuming this will never happen is factually incorrect and risky. Why C fails: Most Salesforce API calls require a version in the URI; omitting it isn't generally how REST/SOAP API versioning works, and it doesn't create some special universal forward compatibility guarantee. Why D fails: Swapping to a completely different API type doesn't eliminate the need to specify and maintain a supported version; Bulk API endpoints are also versioned."),
        Question(id: "117", question: "Custom Metadata Types for environment-specific config An integration's external endpoint URL, API key reference, and batch size differ between the sandbox and production orgs, and the team wants these values to be deployable via standard deployment tools without hardcoding environment-specific values directly in Apex. What should be used?", options: [("A", "Hardcode the production URL and settings directly in the Apex class, then manually edit the code before each sandbox deployment"), ("B", "Custom Metadata Types, which can store environment-specific configuration values (endpoint URL, batch size, etc.) as deployable, queryable records that Apex reads at runtime, allowing per-environment values without code changes"), ("C", "Custom Settings only, since Custom Metadata Types cannot be deployed via standard deployment tools"), ("D", "Store the values only as comments in the Apex class documentation, read manually by developers before each deployment")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Custom Metadata Types are designed exactly for this use case — they store configuration data as metadata records that can be deployed via standard tools (Metadata API/change sets/CI pipelines) and queried by Apex at runtime, letting each environment have its own values without editing code. Why A fails: Hardcoding and manually editing code per environment is exactly the fragile, error-prone practice the requirement is trying to avoid. Why C fails: Custom Metadata Types are in fact deployable via standard deployment tools (Metadata API, packages, change sets) — this is one of their key advantages over Custom Settings, which are more focused on org-specific/hierarchical runtime configuration rather than being metadata deployable in the same way. Why D fails: Comments in Apex are not read or applied automatically by any process; this doesn't provide functional runtime configuration at all."),
        Question(id: "118", question: "An integration relies on a Named Credential and Connected App configured in a sandbox for testing against a mock endpoint. What must the architect plan for when promoting this integration to production?", options: [("A", "Assume the sandbox's Named Credential and Connected App configuration will automatically point to the correct production endpoint and credentials with no changes needed"), ("B", "Explicitly plan to reconfigure (not just deploy as-is) the Named Credential's endpoint URL and authentication details, and the Connected App's settings/consumer keys, for the production environment as part of the promotion process, since these often need environment-specific values"), ("C", "Delete the Connected App and Named Credential entirely in production, since they serve no purpose outside of sandboxes"), ("D", "Rely solely on a full sandbox refresh to automatically carry the correct production values into the org")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Named Credentials and Connected App settings frequently contain environment-specific values (endpoint URLs, auth provider details, consumer keys/secrets) that must be deliberately reconfigured for production as part of a promotion plan, rather than assuming they'll be correct automatically after a simple deployment. Why A fails: Sandbox configurations typically point to test/mock endpoints and sandbox-specific auth setups; assuming no changes are needed risks the integration pointing at the wrong (or nonexistent) production endpoint. Why C fails: Named Credentials and Connected Apps are exactly the components needed in production to run the live integration; deleting them would break the integration entirely. Why D fails: A sandbox refresh moves data/config from production into a sandbox, not the reverse; it does not \"carry\" correct production values into anything and is unrelated to a sandbox-to-production promotion of integration settings."),
        Question(id: "119", question: "An integration's Apex code relies on very specific default field ordering behavior in a JSON serialization that happened to work in one particular Salesforce release but isn't a documented guarantee. What resilience risk should the architect flag ahead of upcoming seasonal releases?", options: [("A", "There is no risk, since Salesforce guarantees all internal serialization behaviors remain byte-for-byte identical forever across every release"), ("B", "Relying on undocumented, non-guaranteed behavior (like incidental field ordering) creates a risk that a future seasonal release could change that behavior and break the integration; the fix is to depend only on documented, guaranteed behavior (e.g., explicitly defining serialization order rather than relying on incidental defaults)"), ("C", "Seasonal releases only affect the Lightning UI and never affect Apex code behavior in any way"), ("D", "This is only a risk for Visualforce pages, not for Apex-based integrations")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Undocumented incidental behaviors (like a particular default serialization order) are not covered by Salesforce's compatibility guarantees, so a seasonal release could legitimately change them without being considered a \"breaking change\" from Salesforce's perspective — the resilient fix is to depend only on explicitly documented, guaranteed behavior. Why A fails: Salesforce does not guarantee that every undocumented internal behavior remains identical across releases; only documented, supported behavior carries that guarantee. Why C fails: Seasonal releases can affect platform behavior broadly, including Apex runtime behavior in various ways, not solely the Lightning UI. Why D fails: This kind of risk is not limited to Visualforce; it applies to any code (including Apex) that depends on undocumented, incidental platform behavior."),
        Question(id: "120", question: "An integration architect is told that the target sandbox will undergo periodic refreshes that temporarily disconnect and reset stored OAuth tokens and Named Credential configurations used by an ongoing testing integration. What should be designed to handle this gracefully?", options: [("A", "Assume sandbox refreshes never affect authentication or Named Credential configuration, so no special handling is needed"), ("B", "Design the integration with error handling and monitoring/alerting that can detect authentication failures after a sandbox refresh (since tokens/configurations may need to be re-established) and includes a documented re-authentication or reconfiguration runbook step, rather than assuming persistent availability"), ("C", "Disable the integration in every sandbox permanently to avoid ever encountering this issue"), ("D", "Refuse to test the integration in any sandbox, testing exclusively in production instead")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Since sandbox refreshes can indeed disrupt OAuth tokens and connected configurations, a resilient design anticipates this with monitoring/alerting to detect the resulting authentication failures and a documented runbook step to re-establish connectivity, rather than assuming uninterrupted availability. Why A fails: Sandbox refreshes are well known to potentially reset or disconnect stored credentials/configurations tied to the refreshed org; assuming no impact is an unsafe assumption. Why C fails: Permanently disabling the integration in sandboxes prevents any testing at all, which isn't a reasonable trade-off for handling a known, manageable disruption. Why D fails: Testing exclusively in production is highly risky and inappropriate for integration validation; sandboxes exist precisely to test safely, and the correct response is to handle the refresh disruption, not avoid sandboxes altogether."),
        Question(id: "121", question: "Apex mock callouts for CI/CD testing A CI/CD pipeline needs to run Apex unit tests for a class that makes an HTTP callout to a partner API, but the tests must not make real live HTTP calls to the partner during automated test runs. What should be used?", options: [("A", "HttpCalloutMock (or StaticResourceCalloutMock) implementations registered via Test.setMock(), allowing Apex tests to simulate the external HTTP response without making a real network call during test execution"), ("B", "Disabling all governor limits during test execution, which automatically converts real callouts into mock calls"), ("C", "Running the tests only manually by a developer who manually confirms the partner API is reachable each time"), ("D", "Removing all @isTest annotations so the tests run as regular Apex classes with live callouts permitted")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Salesforce's Test.setMock() with an HttpCalloutMock (or StaticResourceCalloutMock) implementation is the standard mechanism for simulating HTTP callout responses within Apex tests, allowing full test coverage of callout logic without any real outbound network call during automated test execution. Why B fails: There is no mechanism where \"disabling governor limits\" converts real callouts into mocked ones; Apex tests actually block real callouts by default and require explicit mocking via Test.setMock() to succeed. Why C fails: Manual developer verification defeats the purpose of an automated CI/CD pipeline and doesn't scale or run unattended. Why D fails: Removing @isTest annotations would change the fundamental nature of the test classes and doesn't address the requirement to avoid live callouts during automated testing; in fact, real (non-mocked) callouts are generally disallowed within Apex test execution regardless."),
        Question(id: "122", question: "An integration relies on querying a specific picklist value (\"Pending Review\") on a custom field, and a business admin later removes that value from the picklist during unrelated org configuration changes, silently breaking the integration. What should the architect have designed to catch this?", options: [("A", "Nothing could have been done; metadata changes made by admins can never be detected or tested for"), ("B", "Automated integration tests (or monitoring) that specifically validate dependencies on configuration-sensitive elements like picklist values, run periodically or as part of a deployment pipeline, to detect when an admin's metadata change breaks an assumption the integration relies on"), ("C", "Prevent all admins from ever making any configuration changes to any object going forward"), ("D", "Rely exclusively on Apex governor limits to automatically catch this type of failure")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Building tests/monitoring that explicitly validate integration-critical configuration dependencies (like required picklist values) — run regularly or in a pipeline — is the way to catch silent breakage caused by well-intentioned but disruptive admin metadata changes before it causes a production failure. Why A fails: While Salesforce doesn't natively prevent metadata changes by design, targeted tests/monitoring absolutely can be designed to detect this kind of dependency-breaking change proactively. Why C fails: Completely blocking all future admin configuration changes is an impractical, overly restrictive response that would cripple normal org administration. Why D fails: Governor limits (like SOQL/DML/CPU limits) are unrelated to detecting picklist value removal; they don't serve as a metadata-change detection mechanism."),
        Question(id: "123", question: "A middleware integration calls a downstream Salesforce org that has recently been failing consistently for the last 10 minutes due to an outage. The middleware keeps retrying every request against Salesforce, adding load and delay to every transaction even though Salesforce is currently down. What pattern should be introduced?", options: [("A", "A circuit breaker pattern — after detecting a threshold of consecutive failures, the circuit \"opens\" and the middleware stops sending requests to Salesforce for a cooldown period, failing fast instead, then periodically tests (half-open state) whether Salesforce has recovered before fully resuming traffic"), ("B", "Increasing the number of retry attempts per request indefinitely until Salesforce responds"), ("C", "Removing all error handling so failed requests simply throw unhandled exceptions"), ("D", "Doubling the number of concurrent requests sent to Salesforce to \"push through\" the outage faster")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A circuit breaker detects repeated failures and \"opens\" to stop sending requests to a known-down dependency, failing fast and reducing wasted load/delay, then periodically probes (half-open) to detect recovery before fully resuming traffic — directly solving the described cascading-load problem during an outage. Why B fails: Indefinite retries per request during a known outage keep adding load and delay to every transaction, exactly the problem described, without addressing the root cause. Why C fails: Removing error handling doesn't prevent wasted requests during the outage; it just makes failures less controlled and visible. Why D fails: Increasing concurrent request volume during an active outage adds more load to a system that's already failing, likely worsening recovery time once it does come back."),
        Question(id: "124", question: "KPI/threshold definition for monitoring An architect is defining monitoring KPIs for a new integration between Salesforce and a partner order system. What KPIs should be established as part of the monitoring requirements?", options: [("A", "Only track whether the integration \"ran\" or \"did not run,\" with no other measurements"), ("B", "Define KPIs such as throughput (records/messages processed per interval), error rate (percentage of failed transactions), latency (time from trigger to completion), and queue depth (backlog size), each with defined acceptable thresholds mapped to alerting"), ("C", "Track only the total number of Salesforce licenses consumed by integration users"), ("D", "Track only the color of the \"Setup > Integrations\" icon in Salesforce Setup")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Meaningful integration monitoring requires multiple KPIs — throughput, error rate, latency, and queue depth — each with defined thresholds, so operations teams can detect degradation (slowdowns, rising failures, growing backlogs) before it becomes a full outage. Why A fails: A binary \"ran/did not run\" check misses partial degradation (e.g., high error rates or growing latency) that could indicate a developing problem well before a full failure occurs. Why C fails: License consumption is a commercial/administrative metric unrelated to the operational health or performance of the integration itself. Why D fails: There is no such Setup UI \"color\" metric; this is not a real or meaningful monitoring signal."),
        Question(id: "125", question: "An org has three integrations running concurrently, and the team wants to be alerted before the org exhausts its daily API call allocation, rather than discovering it only after integrations start failing. What should be implemented?", options: [("A", "Do nothing proactive; simply wait for integrations to start failing with API limit errors, then investigate reactively after the fact"), ("B", "Monitor API usage via the org's /limits endpoint (or equivalent usage-tracking tooling) and configure a proactive alert threshold (e.g., at 80% of the daily allocation) so the team is notified before the limit is actually exhausted"), ("C", "Increase the number of Salesforce licenses, which automatically and directly increases the daily API call limit proportionally in real time"), ("D", "Rely exclusively on end users reporting slowness as the sole detection mechanism for API limit issues")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce's /limits REST endpoint (or equivalent monitoring tooling) exposes current API usage against the org's allocation, and setting a proactive threshold alert (e.g., 80%) lets the team intervene before integrations actually start failing due to exhaustion. Why A fails: Waiting for failures to occur before investigating is purely reactive and directly contradicts the stated goal of being alerted \"before\" exhaustion. Why C fails: While license counts and edition can influence API limits in some cases, simply adding licenses doesn't automatically and immediately scale the daily API limit in real time as a monitoring/alerting substitute. Why D fails: Relying solely on end-user complaints is an unreliable, indirect, and delayed signal compared to directly monitoring actual API usage data."),
        Question(id: "126", question: "Event Monitoring / EventLogFile for audit and bottleneck detection A security team suspects that an integration user's API credentials may have been used from an unexpected location at an unusual volume, and also wants to identify which specific API calls are consuming the most time. What Salesforce capability should be used?", options: [("A", "Event Monitoring (EventLogFile data, or real-time Event Monitoring where available), which captures detailed API usage/login event data including origin, volume, and performance-related timing information, useful for both security auditing and performance bottleneck identification"), ("B", "Standard page layouts, since they display API usage information natively"), ("C", "Custom picklist field history tracking, since it records API call performance data"), ("D", "The standard Salesforce login page, since it shows historical API call volumes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Event Monitoring (via EventLogFile analysis or real-time event monitoring) is specifically designed to capture detailed records of API calls, logins, and related activity — including origin, volume, and timing — making it the correct tool for both the security investigation and the performance bottleneck analysis described. Why B fails: Standard page layouts control which fields/related lists display on a record page; they have no capability to surface API usage audit or performance data. Why C fails: Field history tracking records changes to specific field values over time; it has nothing to do with API call auditing or performance timing. Why D fails: The standard login page is a user-facing authentication screen; it does not provide historical API call volume or audit data to administrators."),
        Question(id: "127", question: "Anypoint Monitoring dashboards A MuleSoft-based integration team wants visibility into flow execution times, error rates, and message volumes across all their deployed Salesforce integration flows, ideally without building custom logging infrastructure from scratch. What should be used?", options: [("A", "Anypoint Monitoring, which provides built-in dashboards and alerting for Mule flow execution times, error rates, and message volumes across deployed applications, without requiring the team to build custom logging infrastructure"), ("B", "Salesforce standard Reports and Dashboards, since they natively monitor MuleSoft runtime performance without any additional configuration"), ("C", "Apex Debug Logs, since they capture MuleSoft flow execution metrics directly"), ("D", "Chatter feed tracking on the integration user's profile, since it logs flow performance data automatically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Anypoint Monitoring is MuleSoft's built-in observability tooling, providing out-of-the-box dashboards and alerting for flow execution times, error rates, and message volumes across deployed Mule applications — exactly matching the \"without building custom infrastructure\" requirement. Why B fails: Salesforce Reports and Dashboards operate on Salesforce data/objects; they have no native visibility into MuleSoft runtime execution metrics unless that data is separately captured and stored as Salesforce records. Why C fails: Apex Debug Logs capture Apex execution details within Salesforce; they have no visibility into MuleSoft's own runtime flow execution occurring outside Salesforce. Why D fails: Chatter feed tracking is a social collaboration feature for record-level updates, not a flow execution monitoring or performance metrics tool."),
        Question(id: "128", question: "Platform Event subscriber health checks An external Platform Events subscriber has been silently stalled for the past hour due to an unhandled internal exception, and no one noticed until a customer complained that data seemed stale. What should be implemented to catch this proactively going forward?", options: [("A", "Nothing further is needed, since Platform Event subscribers never require any health monitoring"), ("B", "Implement subscriber health checks that monitor delivery status and replay position (e.g., tracking whether new events are being consumed and whether the ReplayId is advancing over time), with alerting if the subscriber appears stalled, so the team is notified before customers notice stale data"), ("C", "Ask customers to always be the primary mechanism for detecting subscriber outages, since this is the most reliable method"), ("D", "Remove the subscriber entirely so it can never stall again, accepting no external notification of Salesforce changes going forward")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Proactive health checks that track whether the subscriber's ReplayId/consumption position is actually advancing (rather than assuming it's healthy) allow the team to detect a stalled subscriber and alert before the business impact (like stale customer-facing data) becomes visible externally. Why A fails: This scenario is a direct example of why subscriber health monitoring is needed — assuming no monitoring is required directly contradicts the lesson from the incident described. Why C fails: Relying on customer complaints as the detection mechanism is a purely reactive, delayed, and poor-experience approach compared to proactive internal monitoring. Why D fails: Removing the subscriber entirely eliminates the integration's entire purpose rather than fixing the underlying monitoring gap that allowed the stall to go unnoticed."),
        Question(id: "129", question: "Transaction Security / anomalous access detection A compliance team wants Salesforce to automatically detect and respond (e.g., block or require additional verification) when an integration user account suddenly makes an unusually high volume of API calls outside normal business hours, which could indicate a compromised credential. What Salesforce capability fits?", options: [("A", "Transaction Security Policies combined with real-time event monitoring, which can detect anomalous patterns (e.g., unusual API volume or off-hours access) against defined conditions and trigger automated real-time responses (block, notify, require step-up verification)"), ("B", "Standard field history tracking, since it can automatically block suspicious API activity"), ("C", "A simple validation rule on the integration user's profile, since validation rules can detect and block anomalous login patterns"), ("D", "Increasing the integration user's API call limit, since higher limits automatically improve security monitoring")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Transaction Security Policies (built on real-time Event Monitoring data) are specifically designed to detect defined anomalous conditions — like unusual API volume or off-hours activity — and trigger real-time automated responses such as blocking the action, notifying admins, or requiring additional verification. Why B fails: Field history tracking passively records changes to field values over time; it has no capability to detect anomalous API access patterns or trigger any blocking response. Why C fails: Validation rules operate on record save conditions within Salesforce data, not on API call volume/timing patterns at the platform/session level; they are not designed for this kind of anomaly detection. Why D fails: Increasing an API call limit only raises the ceiling before hitting a governor limit; it has nothing to do with detecting or responding to suspicious usage patterns, and in fact could make abuse harder to notice via limit-based signals."),
        Question(id: "130", question: "An integration architect is designing the operational runbook for a new integration and must decide which failures should auto-retry, which should alert operations, and which require manual intervention. What classification approach should be used?", options: [("A", "Escalate every single failure, regardless of type, directly to a senior executive immediately"), ("B", "Classify failures by root cause and impact: transient/system errors auto-retry with backoff and only alert if retries exhaust; application/data errors route to a business/ops queue for correction; and critical/systemic failures (e.g., total endpoint outage) trigger immediate alerting/escalation to on-call operations for manual intervention"), ("C", "Treat all failures identically with no differentiation, retrying every error type indefinitely without ever alerting anyone"), ("D", "Disable all alerting entirely to avoid alert fatigue, regardless of failure severity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A well-designed escalation path tiers failures by root cause and business impact — auto-retrying likely-transient system errors, routing application/data errors for business correction, and reserving immediate operational escalation for critical/systemic failures — ensuring the right response effort matches each failure's actual severity. Why A fails: Escalating every failure to a senior executive immediately, regardless of severity, causes unnecessary alarm and doesn't scale as an operational practice. Why C fails: Retrying every error type indefinitely with no differentiation wastes resources on non-transient application errors and provides no alerting for genuinely critical failures. Why D fails: Disabling all alerting entirely removes any ability to detect and respond to real critical failures, trading alert fatigue for total blindness to problems."),
        Question(id: "131", question: "Outbound Messaging built-in retry window An architect needs to explain to a stakeholder how long Salesforce will keep attempting to redeliver an Outbound Message if the target endpoint isn't acknowledging receipt, before requiring manual intervention. What should be stated?", options: [("A", "Salesforce retries indefinitely forever with no time limit"), ("B", "Salesforce automatically retries delivery of an unacknowledged outbound message for up to 24 hours, after which manual intervention (e.g., reviewing the failed message queue in Setup, or supporting resend approaches) is needed if the endpoint still hasn't acknowledged"), ("C", "Salesforce makes exactly one delivery attempt and never retries under any circumstance"), ("D", "Salesforce retries exactly 3 times regardless of elapsed time, with no other time-based behavior")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Salesforce's Outbound Messaging feature includes a built-in retry window of up to 24 hours for unacknowledged messages; once that window is exhausted without acknowledgment, administrators need to intervene (e.g., via the Setup UI to review/resend as supported). Why A fails: The retry window is bounded (up to 24 hours), not an indefinite/forever retry. Why C fails: Outbound Messaging does have built-in retry behavior beyond a single attempt; this option incorrectly describes it as having no retry at all. Why D fails: The retry behavior is time-window-based (up to 24 hours), not defined as a fixed count of exactly 3 attempts."),
        Question(id: "132", question: "Platform Event replay for recovery after outage A subscriber to a critical Platform Event went offline for 45 minutes due to a deployment issue. The team needs to recover the events published during that window once the subscriber is back online, without re-architecting the whole integration. What recovery procedure should be followed?", options: [("A", "Reconnect the subscriber using its last successfully stored ReplayId, which will cause the Streaming API to redeliver events published from that point forward, recovering the 45-minute gap (within the platform's retention window)"), ("B", "Manually contact Salesforce Support to individually resend each missed event one at a time"), ("C", "Accept that the 45 minutes of events are permanently and unavoidably lost, since Platform Events do not support any replay capability"), ("D", "Restart the subscriber with ReplayId set to only the newest event, ignoring anything published during the outage")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The standard recovery procedure is to reconnect using the subscriber's last known-good ReplayId, which triggers redelivery of everything published since that point (subject to the event retention window), recovering the gap without needing to redesign anything. Why B fails: Salesforce Support does not manually individually resend Platform Events; the reconnect-with-ReplayId mechanism is the self-service, built-in recovery procedure, not a support ticket process. Why C fails: Platform Events do support a built-in replay mechanism via ReplayId specifically to prevent this kind of permanent data loss during subscriber downtime. Why D fails: Setting the ReplayId to only the newest event deliberately skips the 45-minute gap, which is the opposite of the recovery goal — recovering the missed events requires resuming from the point before the outage, not after it."),
        Question(id: "133", question: "Bulk API partial success recovery procedure A recurring nightly Bulk API job to sync 50,000 Contact records fails for 300 records due to a downstream validation rule change made earlier that day, while the other 49,700 succeed. What recovery procedure should the architect define for ongoing operations?", options: [("A", "Automatically and silently discard the 300 failed records every night with no further action or visibility"), ("B", "Define a recovery procedure that captures and reviews the 300 failed records' specific error reasons from the job's result files, determines whether the issue is a source-data problem or a destination-side validation rule change, corrects the root cause (or the source data), and reprocesses just the 300 affected records via idempotent upsert — without re-running the full 50,000-record job unnecessarily"), ("C", "Re-run the entire 50,000-record job from scratch every time any record fails, regardless of whether it was already successfully synced"), ("D", "Ignore the failures indefinitely since 49,700 out of 50,000 (99.4%) is considered acceptable data quality by default")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The right recovery procedure isolates and investigates just the specific failed records via the job's per-record error results, addresses the actual root cause (here, a validation rule change), and reprocesses only the affected 300 records using an idempotent upsert — efficient and targeted rather than wasteful or blind. Why A fails: Silently discarding failures with no visibility or investigation risks masking a real, recurring root cause (like the validation rule change) and losing data indefinitely. Why C fails: Re-running the entire 50,000-record job every time is wasteful and unnecessary when only 300 records actually need correction and reprocessing, especially since idempotent upsert makes targeted reprocessing safe and sufficient. Why D fails: Whether a failure rate is \"acceptable\" isn't a fixed universal threshold like 99.4%; specific validation failures tied to a real root cause (a validation rule change) should be investigated and resolved, not simply tolerated by default."),
        Question(id: "134", question: "A business stakeholder wants a simple, ongoing way to see recent integration execution history (success/failure counts, timestamps, error messages) without needing raw log file access or developer involvement each time. What should the architect propose?", options: [("A", "Direct access to raw Apex Debug Logs for the stakeholder, since they are the best way for a non-technical business user to review integration history"), ("B", "A custom object (e.g., \"Integration Log\") populated by the integration process itself with each run's outcome (status, timestamp, error detail, record counts), which the stakeholder can view directly in Salesforce via list views, reports, or dashboards without needing raw logs or developer help"), ("C", "EventLogFile downloads emailed manually to the stakeholder once a quarter"), ("D", "No solution is possible; business stakeholders can never be given visibility into integration execution history")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A custom logging object populated automatically by the integration gives business stakeholders self-service, Salesforce-native visibility (via list views/reports/dashboards) into execution history, without requiring raw log file parsing or developer intervention for each inquiry. Why A fails: Raw Apex Debug Logs are technical, verbose, and not intended for direct business-stakeholder consumption; they are a poor fit for this specific self-service reporting need. Why C fails: A quarterly manual email of raw EventLogFile data is neither timely (\"ongoing\" visibility) nor easily consumable by a non-technical stakeholder without additional processing. Why D fails: This is factually incorrect — building a custom log object with Reports/Dashboards is a well-established, common Salesforce pattern for exactly this kind of business-facing integration visibility."),
        Question(id: "135", question: "Reports/Dashboards on integration log objects for self-service visibility Following on from a custom \"Integration Log\" object, the business now wants a dashboard showing daily success rates and top error types over the last 30 days, refreshed automatically, without asking IT each time. What should be built?", options: [("A", "A Salesforce Dashboard built on Reports against the Integration Log custom object, with report filters for the last 30 days and groupings by status/error type, scheduled or set to refresh automatically so the business can self-serve this visibility"), ("B", "A one-time static screenshot of the data emailed to the business team, never updated again"), ("C", "A Visualforce page with hardcoded values manually updated by a developer once a month"), ("D", "Direct read access to the underlying database tables for business users to write their own SQL queries")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: A Salesforce Dashboard built on Reports against the Integration Log object, filtered to the last 30 days and grouped by status/error type, provides the exact self-service, automatically refreshed visibility the business wants without ongoing IT involvement. Why B fails: A static one-time screenshot goes stale immediately and provides no ongoing 30-day rolling visibility. Why C fails: A manually updated Visualforce page reintroduces a developer dependency and a monthly (not daily-refreshed) cadence, contradicting the self-service and automatic-refresh requirements. Why D fails: Salesforce doesn't expose its underlying database tables for direct SQL access to business users, and this would bypass Salesforce's security model entirely even if it were possible."),
        Question(id: "136", question: "An integration's daily failure report lumps together records that failed once and then succeeded on auto-retry with records that have failed every single day for two weeks straight. What reporting requirement should the architect define?", options: [("A", "A single combined failure count with no distinction, since all failures are equally important"), ("B", "A reporting requirement that distinguishes transient failures (auto-recovered on retry, requiring no action) from persistent failures (failing repeatedly over time, requiring business escalation), so stakeholders can focus attention on records that actually need intervention"), ("C", "A report that only ever shows the most recent day's data, discarding all historical failure trends"), ("D", "A report that hides all failures from stakeholders to avoid causing concern")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Separating auto-recovered transient failures from records persistently failing over time lets stakeholders correctly prioritize attention on the persistent cases that actually need business escalation, rather than being distracted by noise from self-resolving issues. Why A fails: Lumping both types together obscures which failures actually need human attention versus which resolved themselves automatically. Why C fails: Showing only the most recent day hides the two-week persistent failure pattern that is the actual signal worth escalating. Why D fails: Hiding failures from stakeholders entirely prevents any oversight or escalation of real, ongoing problems."),
        Question(id: "137", question: "An enterprise exposes a single Salesforce API to 8 different internal consumer applications via distinct Connected Apps, and the integration architecture team wants to know which consumers are approaching their allotted share of the org's API budget for capacity planning. What reporting capability should be established?", options: [("A", "A single combined total of all API calls with no breakdown by consumer, since aggregate volume is all that matters"), ("B", "Governance reporting that tracks API usage broken out per Connected App/consumer (e.g., via Event Monitoring API usage data), enforcing or at least visualizing each consumer's share of the org's allocation, to support proactive capacity planning"), ("C", "Deleting seven of the eight Connected Apps so there is only one consumer to track"), ("D", "Relying exclusively on each consuming team to self-report their own usage via an honor system with no platform data involved")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Per-consumer usage reporting (attributable via Connected App identity in Event Monitoring/API usage data) lets the architecture team see which specific consumers are approaching their share of the org's limited API budget, enabling proactive governance and capacity planning rather than reacting only after a limit is exhausted. Why A fails: An aggregate-only total can't reveal which of the 8 consumers is actually driving usage growth, defeating the purpose of per-consumer capacity planning. Why C fails: Deleting Connected Apps removes needed integrations entirely rather than providing the visibility the governance reporting requirement calls for. Why D fails: Self-reported, platform-data-free estimates are unreliable and unverifiable compared to actual usage data captured by the platform itself."),
        Question(id: "138", question: "Bulk API jobs (recovery procedure) A scheduled nightly Bulk API job silently fails to even start (e.g., due to an expired OAuth token on the integration user) rather than partially failing on individual records. What should the recovery/escalation procedure specifically account for, beyond typical per-record error handling?", options: [("A", "Only per-record error handling is needed; a job that fails to start entirely requires no different treatment than individual record failures"), ("B", "A separate monitoring/alerting check for whether the scheduled job executed at all (job-level health check), since a job that never starts produces no per-record errors to review — this requires monitoring the absence of expected execution/completion, not just reviewing result files from a job that did run"), ("C", "Assume the job always starts successfully, since Bulk API jobs cannot fail to start under any circumstances"), ("D", "Wait for the next night's job to see if it succeeds instead, treating a fully missed night as routine and requiring no alert")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A job that fails to start entirely (e.g., due to expired credentials) produces no per-record error data at all, so the monitoring design must separately detect the absence of an expected successful execution — a job-level \"did this run and complete\" health check — rather than relying solely on reviewing failed-record result files. Why A fails: Per-record error handling only works when a job actually executes; it provides no signal at all when the job fails before ever starting. Why C fails: Jobs can and do fail to start for reasons like expired tokens, misconfigured Named Credentials, or connectivity issues; assuming this can't happen ignores a real failure mode. Why D fails: Silently waiting for the next scheduled run without alerting risks an entire missed night's sync going unnoticed and unescalated, which is the opposite of a sound recovery/escalation design."),
        Question(id: "139", question: "Platform Event subscriber health check vs replay position drift An external subscriber's dashboard shows it is \"connected\" to the Streaming API, but its stored ReplayId hasn't advanced in six hours even though new Inventory Changed events are being published continuously. What should this be recognized as, and what should the health check specifically monitor?", options: [("A", "This is normal and healthy behavior, since \"connected\" status alone is sufficient evidence the subscriber is working correctly"), ("B", "This indicates the subscriber is stalled (e.g., stuck in an internal processing loop or silently failing after receiving events) despite showing as \"connected\"; the health check must specifically monitor whether the ReplayId/consumption position is actually advancing over time, not just whether a connection is open"), ("C", "This indicates a Salesforce platform-wide outage affecting all Streaming API subscribers globally"), ("D", "This indicates the org's Platform Events hourly publishing limit has been exceeded, blocking delivery entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A live connection status alone doesn't guarantee the subscriber is actually making progress; the ReplayId not advancing despite continuous publishing is a clear signal of a stalled subscriber, and the correct health check design must specifically track consumption/progress, not just connectivity status. Why A fails: \"Connected\" only confirms the streaming connection is open; it says nothing about whether the subscriber's processing logic is actually consuming and advancing through events. Why C fails: A stalled ReplayId on one specific subscriber while events continue being published is a subscriber-side issue, not evidence of a platform-wide outage affecting all subscribers. Why D fails: Hitting the publishing limit would affect the publisher's ability to publish new events, not cause an already-connected subscriber's replay position to stop advancing while new events are, per the scenario, still being published."),
        Question(id: "140", question: "A services team must periodically report to a customer on whether a contractual SLA (95% of orders synced within 15 minutes) was met each month. What reporting design should the architect specify?", options: [("A", "A report that only shows whether the integration ran at all each day, with no timing or percentage-based SLA calculation"), ("B", "A report/dashboard built on captured per-transaction timestamps (start and completion time) that calculates the percentage of transactions meeting the 15-minute threshold over a selected period, trending it monthly to demonstrate ongoing SLA compliance"), ("C", "A manual, ad hoc spreadsheet recreated from scratch by a developer each month with no repeatable underlying data source"), ("D", "A report that shows only the single slowest transaction of the month, ignoring the aggregate percentage entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: To demonstrate compliance with a percentage-based SLA over time, the integration must capture per-transaction timing data and the report must calculate the percentage meeting the threshold across a period, trended monthly — directly matching the stated contractual reporting need. Why A fails: A simple ran/did-not-run check provides no visibility into the actual 15-minute timing threshold or the required 95% compliance percentage. Why C fails: A manually recreated, non-repeatable spreadsheet is error-prone, inconsistent, and doesn't scale as a reliable ongoing SLA reporting mechanism. Why D fails: Showing only the single worst transaction ignores the percentage-based nature of the SLA (95% of orders), which requires an aggregate calculation across all transactions, not a single outlier."),
        Question(id: "141", question: "An organization runs a mixed integration landscape: some flows in MuleSoft, some in native Salesforce (Apex/Flow), and some via Platform Events. Leadership wants a single, consistent view of overall integration health rather than needing to check three separate tools every time. What should the architect propose?", options: [("A", "Refuse the request, since it is technically impossible to correlate monitoring data across different integration technologies"), ("B", "Establish a consolidated monitoring/reporting layer (e.g., feeding key metrics from Anypoint Monitoring, Salesforce Event Monitoring, and Platform Event subscriber health checks into a shared dashboard or reporting object) so leadership gets a unified health view without manually checking three separate tools"), ("C", "Migrate every single integration to only one of the three technologies immediately, regardless of fit, purely to simplify monitoring"), ("D", "Tell leadership to check each of the three tools separately every day indefinitely, since no consolidation is possible")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: While the underlying technologies differ, their respective monitoring data (Anypoint Monitoring metrics, Salesforce Event Monitoring data, Platform Event subscriber health) can be aggregated into a shared reporting layer or dashboard, giving leadership the requested unified view without eliminating the appropriate mix of technologies. Why A fails: Cross-tool metric consolidation into a shared reporting layer is a standard, achievable practice, not a technical impossibility. Why C fails: Forcing every integration onto a single technology purely to simplify monitoring ignores the fact that each technology was presumably chosen because it fit specific use cases, and this is a disproportionate response to a reporting need. Why D fails: This ignores the actual request (a unified view) and simply reasserts the status quo problem the architect was asked to solve."),
        Question(id: "142", question: "After several months of only discovering integration problems when customers complain, leadership asks the architect to shift the team's monitoring approach. What philosophy change should be recommended?", options: [("A", "Continue exactly as before, since customer complaints are an acceptable and sufficient detection mechanism for integration issues"), ("B", "Shift toward proactive monitoring — defined KPI thresholds, automated alerting on error rate/latency/queue depth/API usage, and subscriber/job health checks — so problems are detected and addressed before they reach customer-visible impact, rather than relying on customer complaints as the primary detection method"), ("C", "Remove all monitoring entirely, since it hasn't been catching problems anyway"), ("D", "Only monitor once per quarter during a scheduled review, since more frequent monitoring is unnecessary")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: The core shift needed is from reactive (customer-complaint-driven) to proactive detection — defining KPI thresholds and automated alerting across error rate, latency, queue depth, API usage, and subscriber/job health — so issues are caught and addressed internally before they become customer-visible problems. Why A fails: This is precisely the reactive approach that has already been shown to fail, letting problems persist until customers notice and complain. Why C fails: Removing monitoring entirely would make detection purely reactive (or nonexistent), moving further from the proactive goal, not closer to it. Why D fails: Quarterly-only checks would leave long gaps where real-time or near-real-time issues go undetected for months, failing to address the timeliness problem at the root of the original complaint-driven pattern."),
        Question(id: "143", question: "Event Monitoring with Transaction Security for layered detection A security team wants both retrospective audit capability (to investigate an incident after the fact) and real-time automated response (to block a suspicious action as it happens) for API-based integration access. What combination should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Only Transaction Security Policies, since they alone provide both real-time blocking and comprehensive historical audit trail analysis"), ("B", "Only EventLogFile-based Event Monitoring, since it alone provides both real-time blocking and retrospective audit capability"), ("C", "A combination of Event Monitoring (EventLogFile data for retrospective audit/analysis of historical API activity) and Transaction Security Policies (built on real-time event data to detect and immediately respond to defined anomalous conditions), since each serves a distinct but complementary purpose"), ("D", "Neither capability is relevant to API-based integration security monitoring")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why C: Event Monitoring (via EventLogFile) is oriented toward retrospective analysis of historical activity for investigations, while Transaction Security Policies act on real-time event data to detect and immediately respond to anomalies as they occur — together they cover both the retrospective and real-time requirements the security team described. Why A fails: Transaction Security Policies are focused on real-time detection/response; they are not the primary tool for deep retrospective historical audit analysis, which EventLogFile-based Event Monitoring is better suited for. Why B fails: EventLogFile-based Event Monitoring is primarily retrospective (log files generated after the fact); it does not provide real-time blocking/response capability on its own. Why D fails: Both capabilities are directly relevant and purpose-built for exactly this kind of API/integration security monitoring scenario."),
        Question(id: "144", question: "An architect is finalizing monitoring thresholds for an integration and wants error rate alerts to avoid both alert fatigue (from over-alerting on minor blips) and dangerous silence (from under-alerting on real degradation). What approach should be used?", options: [("A", "Alert immediately on every single individual failure, no matter how isolated, treating every failure as equally urgent"), ("B", "Never alert on error rate at all, since any threshold will inevitably be imperfect"), ("C", "Define tiered thresholds (e.g., a lower \"warning\" threshold for elevated but non-critical error rates routed to a non-urgent channel, and a higher \"critical\" threshold for sustained/severe error rates triggering immediate on-call paging), calibrated against the integration's normal baseline behavior"), ("D", "Set the alert threshold at exactly 100% failure rate only, ignoring any partial degradation below that")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why C: Tiered thresholds — a lower warning level for early signs of trouble routed less urgently, and a higher critical level triggering immediate paging — calibrated to the integration's actual baseline behavior, directly balances the two competing risks of alert fatigue and dangerous silence. Why A fails: Alerting urgently on every single isolated failure, including normal transient blips, is exactly the recipe for the alert fatigue the architect is trying to avoid. Why B fails: Not alerting at all on error rate reintroduces the \"dangerous silence\" risk, missing real degradation entirely. Why D fails: Waiting for 100% total failure before alerting means severe but partial degradation (e.g., 60% failure rate) would go unnoticed until the situation is already at its worst."),
        Question(id: "145", question: "SLA-impacting vs non-impacting failures A weekly integration report currently shows a raw failure count with no other context, and the business struggles to tell whether a given week's failures actually caused any customer-facing SLA breach. What should the architect add to the reporting design?", options: [("A", "Nothing; a raw failure count is sufficient context for any stakeholder to assess SLA impact"), ("B", "A reporting dimension that correlates each failure with whether it caused an actual SLA breach (e.g., whether the record was eventually resolved within the SLA window via retry, versus one that missed the window entirely), so stakeholders can distinguish failures with real customer impact from ones that self-resolved in time"), ("C", "A report that stops counting failures altogether, showing only total volume processed"), ("D", "A report that assumes every single failure, regardless of retry outcome, breached the SLA by default")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Adding a dimension that ties each failure to its actual SLA outcome (resolved-in-time via retry vs. genuinely missed the window) lets stakeholders distinguish real customer-impacting breaches from failures that were transient and self-resolved, which a raw count cannot do. Why A fails: A raw count treats a failure that retried successfully within the SLA window identically to one that caused a real breach, providing no way to assess actual customer impact. Why C fails: Removing failure visibility entirely eliminates the ability to assess SLA impact at all, the opposite of what's needed. Why D fails: Assuming every failure is a breach by default, without checking whether a retry resolved it within the window, overstates the actual customer impact and could trigger unnecessary escalations."),
        Question(id: "146", question: "Historical integration reports show API call volume growing 20% quarter-over-quarter for the past year, driven by organic business growth. What should the architect do with this reporting data from a maintenance perspective?", options: [("A", "Ignore the trend entirely, since past growth has no bearing on future capacity needs"), ("B", "Use the historical growth trend to proactively forecast when the org's daily API allocation will likely be exhausted at the current growth rate, and plan ahead (e.g., optimizing call efficiency, migrating some integrations to event-driven patterns, or evaluating an API allocation add-on) before the limit is actually hit"), ("C", "Wait until the org actually hits its daily API limit and integrations start failing, then react at that point"), ("D", "Immediately double the number of Salesforce licenses purchased, since license count is the only lever that affects API capacity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Reporting data showing a consistent growth trend should be used proactively for capacity planning — forecasting when current usage patterns will exhaust the API allocation and taking mitigating action (efficiency improvements, event-driven redesign, or allocation add-ons) before that point is reached, which is a core purpose of integration reporting for maintenance. Why A fails: Ignoring a clear, consistent growth trend abandons the entire point of collecting historical usage data for planning purposes. Why C fails: Waiting until the limit is actually hit is a reactive approach that risks integration failures and business disruption that proactive planning is meant to avoid. Why D fails: License count is not the only lever affecting API capacity, and assuming so without considering call-efficiency or architectural changes ignores other equally valid (and often cheaper) mitigation options."),
        Question(id: "147", question: "An integration starts failing entirely, and initial triage shows the partner's endpoint is returning HTTP 503 for every request, with Salesforce-side configuration unchanged. What should the escalation procedure specify for this scenario?", options: [("A", "Immediately begin reconfiguring or redeploying the Salesforce-side integration components, assuming the problem must be on the Salesforce side"), ("B", "Escalate to the partner's operational/support channel as an externally-caused outage (since 503 responses indicate a server-side issue on their end), while internally monitoring for recovery and considering circuit-breaker/backoff behavior to reduce load during the outage, rather than assuming an internal misconfiguration"), ("C", "Permanently disable the integration and remove all related Salesforce configuration, since the partner's outage means the integration is no longer needed"), ("D", "Repeatedly redeploy the exact same unchanged Salesforce configuration every few minutes until the 503 errors stop")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: HTTP 503 responses (Service Unavailable) with unchanged Salesforce-side configuration point to a server-side problem on the partner's end, so the correct escalation path is to engage the partner's support channel as an external outage, while applying resilience patterns (backoff/circuit breaker) internally to reduce added load during their recovery. Why A fails: Reconfiguring the Salesforce side addresses a problem that, based on the evidence (unchanged config, 503 from partner), doesn't originate there, wasting effort and potentially introducing unrelated changes. Why C fails: A temporary partner-side outage doesn't mean the integration is permanently unneeded; removing all configuration is a drastic, irreversible overreaction to what is likely a transient issue. Why D fails: Repeatedly redeploying identical, unchanged configuration cannot fix a problem that lives on the partner's server, and doing so repeatedly adds unnecessary operational noise without addressing the root cause."),
        Question(id: "148", question: "An integration that has been running for three years starts failing, and the operations team spends two days just trying to figure out who owns it and who to contact, since the original developer left the company. What should the architect ensure exists as part of ongoing integration governance and reporting?", options: [("A", "Nothing; ownership documentation is not something integration governance needs to track"), ("B", "A maintained integration inventory/registry (e.g., part of governance reporting) documenting each integration's business owner, technical owner/support contact, purpose, and dependencies, kept up to date as personnel and systems change, so operational issues can be triaged and escalated quickly"), ("C", "Rely solely on institutional memory among remaining staff, with no written documentation of ownership"), ("D", "Assume every integration is owned by whoever happens to be on-call at the moment a failure occurs, regardless of actual expertise")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: A maintained integration inventory documenting business/technical ownership, purpose, and dependencies — kept current as staff and systems change — is exactly what prevents the kind of costly ownership-discovery delay described, enabling fast triage and escalation when problems occur. Why A fails: This scenario is a direct illustration of why ownership tracking matters; treating it as unnecessary ignores the real operational cost just experienced. Why C fails: Institutional memory is fragile and exactly what failed here once the original developer left; undocumented tribal knowledge is not a reliable governance practice. Why D fails: Assigning ownership arbitrarily to whoever is on-call, regardless of actual system knowledge, does not solve the expertise/context gap and could lead to poor troubleshooting decisions."),
        Question(id: "149", question: "A regulator requires evidence that all financial data synced between Salesforce and a partner system over the past year was transmitted securely and that any failed transmissions were properly tracked and resolved. What should the architect ensure the integration's reporting/logging captures to support this audit?", options: [("A", "Nothing beyond current application logs is needed, since regulators only ever ask about very recent activity"), ("B", "An auditable historical record (e.g., retained integration logs/reports) capturing transmission timestamps, security context (e.g., confirmation of TLS/mTLS usage where applicable), success/failure status, and resolution details for failures, retained for the required period to demonstrate compliant handling over time"), ("C", "Only a live dashboard showing the current day's status, since historical data is irrelevant to regulatory audits"), ("D", "Verbal assurance from the integration team that everything was handled correctly, with no supporting documentation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Regulatory audits typically require durable, retained evidence — timestamps, security context, and failure resolution details over the audit period — so the integration's logging/reporting design must be built to retain and produce this historical evidence, not just reflect current-moment status. Why A fails: Regulatory audits frequently examine activity over an extended historical period (e.g., a full year, as stated), not just recent activity, so retention design matters. Why C fails: A live, current-day-only dashboard cannot demonstrate compliant handling across the entire audit period the regulator is asking about. Why D fails: Verbal assurance with no supporting documented evidence would not satisfy a regulator's evidentiary requirements for a compliance audit."),
        Question(id: "150", question: "After a year of operating an integration with mature monitoring, alerting, and reporting in place, the architect wants to use the accumulated data to actually improve the integration going forward, not just keep it running. What should be done with the reporting data?", options: [("A", "Archive the reporting data with no further analysis, since its only purpose was historical compliance record-keeping"), ("B", "Periodically analyze accumulated monitoring/reporting data (error patterns, recurring root causes, latency trends, capacity trends) to identify systemic improvement opportunities — such as redesigning a fragile pattern, adjusting thresholds, or renegotiating a partner SLA — feeding operational learnings back into the integration's ongoing architecture and design"), ("C", "Discard all reporting data older than one week, since only the most recent data could ever be useful"), ("D", "Use the reporting data exclusively to assign individual blame for past failures, with no forward-looking design changes considered")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why B: Mature integration maintenance treats accumulated monitoring/reporting data as an input to continuous improvement — analyzing recurring patterns and root causes to drive concrete architectural or process changes — rather than letting the data sit unused once initial compliance or operational goals are met. Why A fails: Treating the data as purely archival ignores its value for identifying and acting on recurring patterns that could meaningfully improve the integration. Why C fails: Discarding older data prevents the kind of longer-term trend analysis (e.g., quarter-over-quarter growth, recurring seasonal patterns) that is often the most valuable input for architectural improvement decisions. Why D fails: Using the data purely for blame rather than forward-looking design or process improvement wastes its main practical value and doesn't address the root causes behind recurring failures."),
        Question(id: "151", question: "authentication/authorization evaluation (8% weighting) A mid-market manufacturer runs a nightly inventory reconciliation job on an AWS-hosted service account that calls the Salesforce REST API. There is no human available to approve access at runtime, and unlike a similar JWT-based integration the team already runs elsewhere, this AWS service has no facility to generate or sign a JWT with a private key — it can only present a pre-issued client ID and client secret it retrieves from a secrets vault. Which OAuth flow should the architect select?", options: [("A", "OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials flow, authenticating as a dedicated integration user via the service's client ID/secret with no browser redirect or JWT signing required"), ("B", "OAuth 2.0 JWT Bearer flow, since it is the only flow Salesforce supports for unattended server-to-server access"), ("C", "OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow, since it provides the strongest security guarantee for any backend service account"), ("D", "OAuth 2.0 Device Flow, since the AWS service has no browser and needs a code-based approval step")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: The Client Credentials flow is designed exactly for this case — a system-level integration with no user interaction and no capability to sign a JWT, authenticating purely with a client ID and secret to represent a dedicated integration user (or connected app identity). Why B fails: JWT Bearer requires the calling system to generate and sign a JWT assertion with a private key; the scenario explicitly states this AWS service has no such capability, so JWT Bearer cannot be implemented here even though it's a valid unattended flow in other cases. Why C fails: Web Server flow requires a live browser redirect and interactive user consent, which is unavailable for an unattended nightly job with no human present. Why D fails: Device Flow exists for input-constrained devices that still have a human available to complete approval on a separate browser; this is a headless backend service with no such human-in-the-loop step described."),
        Question(id: "152", question: "authentication/authorization evaluation (8% weighting) A global insurer's identity team already federates employee identity to dozens of internal applications through a corporate Identity Provider using SAML assertions, and wants newly onboarded employees to be automatically signed into Salesforce using their existing corporate credentials with no separate Salesforce username/password ever created. Separately, a mobile field-inspection app needs to call the Salesforce REST API on behalf of the same employees to submit inspection photos, with the employee explicitly granting consent the first time they use the app. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Configure Salesforce as a SAML Service Provider (SP) consuming assertions from the corporate IdP for employee login, and use the OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow for the mobile app's delegated per-user API access"), ("B", "Configure Salesforce as a SAML Identity Provider (IdP) issuing assertions to the corporate IdP for employee login, and use the OAuth 2.0 JWT Bearer flow for the mobile app's per-user API access"), ("C", "Use OAuth 2.0 for both employee login and the mobile app's API access, since SAML cannot federate identity for interactive human logins"), ("D", "Configure Salesforce as a SAML Service Provider (SP) for employee login, and use SAML Bearer assertions for the mobile app's API access, since SAML Bearer inherently includes an interactive consent screen")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why A: Since the corporate IdP is the existing source of federated identity, Salesforce should act as the SAML Service Provider consuming assertions for SSO login; for the mobile app's delegated, per-user, consent-driven API access, the OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow is the correct fit because it is built around an interactive browser-based consent screen tied to a specific user. Why B fails: This reverses the SAML roles — the corporate IdP, not Salesforce, is the existing identity source for employee login, so Salesforce must be the SP, not the IdP, in this relationship; it also selects JWT Bearer, which has no interactive consent screen and doesn't match the \"employee explicitly granting consent\" requirement. Why C fails: SAML is specifically designed for federated interactive human login (SSO) and is the appropriate mechanism here; dismissing it entirely for login ignores the insurer's existing SAML federation investment described in the scenario. Why D fails: SAML Bearer flow is used for pre-established trust/assertion exchange without an interactive consent screen, not for scenarios requiring the user to explicitly grant consent on first use."),
        Question(id: "153", question: "system and process constraint evaluation (22% weighting) A distributor's Salesforce-to-ERP credit memo integration is designed to fire immediately whenever a Case is marked \"Resolved – Refund Approved,\" pushing the refund to the ERP in real time. During requirements validation, the finance team reveals that every refund over $500 must first pass through a multi-step Salesforce approval process before the Case can reach that status, and approvers are frequently unavailable for 24–48 hours, while the ERP integration user's session-based Named Credential token expires after 12 hours of inactivity. What should the architect flag as the primary constraint requiring a design change?", options: [("A", "The Bulk API's 2,000-record threshold, since the integration should be redesigned to batch all refunds above $500 into a single nightly Bulk API job regardless of approval status"), ("B", "The 100-callout-per-transaction Apex governor limit, since each approval step increments the transaction's callout count until the limit is exceeded"), ("C", "The approval process is an asynchronous business-process constraint that can introduce multi-day delays before the triggering status is ever reached, and the Named Credential's inactivity-based token expiration must be designed to refresh (e.g., via a valid OAuth refresh cycle) rather than assumed to remain valid across that variable delay"), ("D", "Change Data Capture's event retention window, since CDC events for the Case object will expire before the approval process completes and the refund event will be permanently lost")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why C: The approval process is a business-process constraint entirely independent of the integration's technical trigger design — it can delay the Case ever reaching \"Resolved – Refund Approved\" by days, and any credential (like a Named Credential relying on an OAuth token) used by the eventual callout must be designed to survive or refresh across that unpredictable delay rather than assuming continuous recent activity. Why A fails: Batching refunds into a nightly job regardless of approval status would push unapproved refunds to the ERP, violating the business requirement that approval must complete first — volume threshold isn't the relevant constraint here. Why B fails: Apex approval process steps do not consume Apex callouts each time they advance; the 100-callout limit is a per-transaction Apex governor limit unrelated to multi-day approval workflow delays. Why D fails: The integration in this design is triggered by a Case status change (e.g., via a flow or trigger reacting to the field update), not by consuming CDC events from the moment the Case was first created, so CDC's retention window is not the limiting factor described."),
        Question(id: "154", question: "security solution for inbound integrations (23% weighting) A healthcare SaaS partner's mobile application will call Salesforce's REST API on behalf of individual clinicians, and the security team has two requirements: (1) only clinicians whose access has been explicitly pre-approved by a Salesforce admin may ever obtain a token through this Connected App, regardless of whether they attempt to self-authorize, and (2) if a clinician's device is lost or stolen, any long-lived refresh token issued to that device must stop working automatically after 14 days of the device not being used, without an admin having to manually revoke it. Which two Connected App configurations should the architect apply? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Set the OAuth policy to \"Admin approved users are pre-authorized\" and assign only the appropriate profiles or permission sets to the Connected App"), ("B", "Set the OAuth policy to \"All users may self-authorize\" combined with a strict IP relaxation policy scoped to clinician office networks"), ("C", "Configure the Connected App's refresh token policy to expire the refresh token after a specified period of inactivity, set to 14 days"), ("D", "Enable a Session Security Level policy on the associated profile requiring High Assurance for all API logins"), ("E", "Configure the refresh token policy to expire the token immediately after each single use (\"expire immediately\")"), ("F", "Enable IP Relaxation to \"Relax IP restrictions\" org-wide for the Connected App to simplify mobile device onboarding")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why A is correct: \"Admin approved users are pre-authorized\" is the specific OAuth policy that blocks any user from obtaining a token through the Connected App unless an admin has explicitly assigned their profile or permission set, directly satisfying requirement (1). Why C is correct: A refresh token policy configured to expire after a set period of inactivity (14 days) automatically invalidates a dormant token from a lost/stolen device without requiring manual admin revocation, directly satisfying requirement (2). Why B fails: \"All users may self-authorize\" allows any user to grant themselves access without admin pre-approval, which is the opposite of requirement (1), and IP relaxation addresses network origin, not user pre-authorization. Why D fails: Session Security Level/High Assurance policies govern step-up authentication strength for sensitive actions; they don't control which users can obtain a token through the Connected App or expire dormant refresh tokens after a set inactivity window. Why E fails: Expiring the refresh token after every single use would break the mobile app's normal continued-use pattern (it would force re-authentication on every session), which is a far more disruptive behavior than the 14-day inactivity requirement actually calls for. Why F fails: Relaxing IP restrictions org-wide widens, rather than narrows, the attack surface and has no bearing on either the pre-authorization or the dormant-token-expiration requirements."),
    ]
}
